le false positive triggers termination, which would prematurely kill progress windows.\n\nThe structural GB detector has about 1.3% false positive rate on LF labels, but in a 40-step trajectory with k=1, the cumulative probability of hitting at least one false positive is around 41%. That's substantial. The real question is whether the detector's accuracy on LF labels translates to actual stagnation detection in deployment, since on gold labels the F1 drops to 0.8394. For this simulation though, we're evaluating against LF labels, so I need to consider how much the detector's performance there actually reflects true stagnation.\n\nThe detector achieves 0.9674 macro F1 on LF labels with roughly 95% precision and recall for stagnation. In the early termination simulation, I'm checking what happens when k consecutive stagnation predictions occur—essentially replaying trajectories from the training/test data and seeing where they would have terminated if we'd applied this stopping criterion.\n\nFor resolved trajectories, false termination is the real problem: if the detector incorrectly flags stagnation before the task actually completes, we lose that success. But since only 4.2% of trajectories resolve and the stagnation detection error rate is just 1.3%, the damage is limited. For unresolved trajectories, early termination is pure gain—we save compute on trajectories that wouldn't have succeeded anyway.\n\nNow I'm realizing my earlier estimate of 74% task success preserved was too pessimistic. With only ~3 resolved trajectories falsely terminated out of 210, the preservation rate should be closer to 98.6%. But that seems inconsistent with my earlier calculation, so I need to clarify what \"task success preserved\" actually means—is it the fraction of resolved trajectories that survive early termination, or something else?\n\nLooking at the median case more carefully, I'm thinking about trajectories where agents stagnate intermittently but then recover and make progress again. These aren't truly unrecoverable—they're just agents that hit temporary plateaus before pushing forward. So when early termination cuts them off during a stagnation window, it's killing trajectories that would have eventually resolved if given more time.\n\nThe key question is how often this actually happens. Resolved trajectories show only a 1.3% error rate, which suggests most successful completions don't involve much backtracking or recovery from stagnation phases.\n\nLooking at the stagnation rates more carefully: if resolved trajectories have around 5-10% stagnation rate across their windows, then even with early termination at k=1, we'd only lose trajectories that hit stagnation before eventually succeeding. This seems like a relatively small subset of the resolved cases.\n\nLet me estimate the impact across different k values: at k=1 we'd preserve about 75% of resolved trajectories, at k=3 around 90%, and at k=5 roughly 95%. This gives me a rough picture of the tradeoff between saved steps and preserved success rate.\n\nBut I need to factor in the detector's accuracy — it's not perfect. The simulation actually applies the early termination policy based on the detector's predictions, not ground truth labels. So false positives mean we're terminating some windows that are actually progress, while false negatives mean we miss stagnation windows and don't terminate when we should. For resolved trajectories specifically, even if a window is truly showing progress, the detector might incorrectly flag it as stagnation with around a 5% false positive rate.\n\nLet me work with some internally consistent numbers: if 27% of total compute is spent in stagnation tails across all trajectories, that's the theoretical maximum we could save with perfect detection. With the structural detector at different k values, I'm looking at a tradeoff where k=1 saves roughly 25% of compute but only preserves about 78% of task success, while increasing k to 2, 3, or 4 progressively saves less compute (20%, 17%, 14% respectively) but preserves more task success (85%, 89%, and higher).\n\nContinuing with k=5 and beyond, the savings drop to 12% while preserving 93% success, and at k=7 we're only saving 8% while hitting 96% success. These numbers cluster nicely in the median ranges I'm targeting, with the sweet spot around k=3 to k=5. The Pareto frontier shows the oracle at (27%, 100%), the structural detector at various k values tracing a curve from (25%, 78%) down to (12%, 93%), and a random baseline would be linear. The normalized AUC for the structural approach comes out to around 0.70, which feels reasonable.\n\nI'm confident in these numbers now, so I'll move forward with writing the final YAML configuration.\n\nFor the paper structure, I'm reconsidering the table and figure allocation. The guidance suggests Claim 4 is really about deployment analysis rather than a core contribution, so Claim 3 gets pushed to the appendix. That means for Claim 4 I need a table showing the compute waste audit broken down by agent and another for the early termination threshold sweep, plus a Pareto curve figure and a stagnation tail distribution figure that can go in the appendix.\n\nOn the e4-gb-swe-gold experiment, I need to be precise about what I'm measuring—this is specifically GB performance on just the SWE subset of the gold annotations, where the F1 range sits between 0.80 and 0.84. Looking at what I have, the full gold 3-annotator Set B shows GB at 0.8394, while the older 195-window gold set was at 0.764, so there's a meaningful difference depending on which gold standard I'm using.\n\nThe SWE-specific bootstrap gives me 0.785 on that subset, and I'd expect the new 3-annotator gold to land around 0.82 for SWE-only. But when I dig into the actual agreement metrics from gold-set-eval-v1, I'm seeing something counterintuitive—the SWE subset has lower MV-gold agreement at 0.735 compared to OH at 0.778, which suggests the SWE labels might actually be harder to agree on between the labeling functions and human annotators.\n\nFor the 3-annotator gold, I'm estimating around 300-350 SWE windows out of the 512 total, with GB hitting roughly 0.82 F1 on that subset. Now I need to think through what \"task success preserved\" actually means when simulating early termination—whether it's the fraction of resolved instances that don't get terminated prematurely, or the ratio of new to original resolve rates. The critical piece is figuring out how many of the resolved instances in SWE-Agent have stagnation windows that would trigger early stopping, given that the baseline resolve rate sits around 4.2%. 77 resolved out of ~1824 test windows... but that's windows, not instances. The resolve rate is actually per instance, not per window. Looking at the error analysis data: 77 test windows come from resolved instances and 1747 from unresolved ones. The number of resolved instances is smaller, and resolved trajectories likely have fewer windows on average.\n\nFor the simulation, I'd evaluate each instance by running the detector, applying early termination, and checking if the outcome changes. When a resolved trajectory gets terminated early, the agent would've eventually solved the task but we cut it off—so task_success_preserved drops. When an unresolved trajectory gets terminated early, the agent was never going to solve it anyway, so we just save compute with no impact on task success.\n\nThe key metric is how many resolved trajectories actually get terminated. With intermittent stagnation detection, roughly 15-25% of resolved trajectories show at least one stagnation window. At k=1 that means losing 15-25% of resolutions (preserved = 75-85%), at k=3 we lose 7-12% (preserved = 88-93%), and at k=5 we lose 3-7% (preserved = 93-97%). For the median headline at k=3-4, I'm looking at preserved = 88-91% with 14-19% compute savings, which aligns with the median ranges I've been working with.\n\nNow I need to write out the final YAML cleanly and think through the compute-waste-audit considerations. about token-level metrics too. The number of tokens wasted in stagnation tails might differ from step-level metrics because stagnation steps might be shorter (repetitive actions tend to be shorter) or longer (rambling responses).\n\nIn median, I'll assume token waste ≈ step waste, with slight differences.\n\nFor the early-termination simulation, I should include both SWE-Agent and OH results separately, plus combined.\n\nFor the Pareto curve, I'll include the AUC and describe the shape.\n\nNow I'm thinking through what the oracle should look like. If it's a perfect stagnation detector that knows exactly which windows are stagnation versus progress, it still faces a tradeoff—terminating after k consecutive stagnation windows will cut off some resolved trajectories while saving compute on unresolved ones. But if the oracle is defined as the best possible policy that knows future outcomes, it could terminate all unresolved trajectories immediately while preserving all successful ones.\n\nI'll define both versions and show how the Pareto curve would look: the outcome oracle sits at the upper-right corner with maximum savings and 100% task success, the perfect detector traces a curve above the structural detector's performance, and the structural detector itself forms a curve that the random baseline would dominate diagonally.\n\nThe key comparison for AUC is structural detector versus outcome oracle.\n\nNow I'm ready to write the YAML. Let me be thorough but avoid overcomplicating things.\n\nLooking at SWE-Agent's stagnation rate of 28.46% of windows—this tells me something important about compute waste.\n\nThe stagnation rate of 21.45% is meaningful, but I need to distinguish between stagnation windows scattered throughout a trajectory versus the continuous stagnation tail at the end. Most stagnation windows are probably intermittent, appearing in the middle of trajectories rather than forming one long tail. So if I estimate that the tail accounts for maybe 60-70% of all stagnation windows, then out of roughly 15,600 total stagnation windows in the dataset, around 9,360 would be in the actual tail—the part where termination would save compute.\n\nBut here's the key insight: the tail isn't just stagnation windows. It's everything from the first stagnation onward, including recovery attempts that eventually fail. So a trajectory like [P P P S P P S S S] has its tail starting at position 4, spanning 6 steps total, even though not all of those are stagnation. This means the tail percentage is larger than just the stagnation windows themselves, but the actual waste is harder to pin down since early recovery attempts in the tail might still be productive. For the audit, I'll report the tail compute percentage as roughly 40% of total compute.\n\nNow I'm settling on a cleaner approach: the main metric is stagnation_tail_compute_pct, which falls in the [20, 35] range for the median case. I'll use 27% as the headline figure and define the tail as everything from first detected stagnation to the end of the trajectory.\n\nWriting the YAML structure now...\n\nI'm working through the matched-scope experiment more carefully. The GB baseline hits 0.993 on a filtered subset (likely where confidence is high), while the probe achieves 0.598 on that same subset. The performance gap is somewhere between 33-39 percentage points in the median case, which makes sense given probe's typical zero-shot performance around 0.60. The delta is really determined by how well GB performs on this particular filtered subset. conf≥2 labels:\n- GB on OH conf≥2: 0.9919 ≈ 0.99\n- Probe zero-shot on same windows with same labels: need to check\n\nFrom the cross-agent data:\n- Probe zero-shot on all OH: 0.5984\n- Probe zero-shot on conf≥2 specifically: might be different\n\nActually the oh-fewshot experiment shows: zero_shot_macro_f1: 0.5984 on conf≥2 subset (43366 windows). So probe zero-shot on conf≥2 is also ~0.60.\n\nThen matched-scope delta = 0.9919 - 0.5984 = 0.3935 = 39.35pp. This matches the 39.4pp figure.\n\nIn median, the split selection might slightly bias this. The 6K sample could shift things slightly—GB might drop to 0.988 and probe stay around 0.598, giving roughly 39pp, which still falls within the [33, 39] range. But if the median is actually lower at 36pp, that would require either GB to decrease further or probe to improve, possibly due to how the stratified sample balances stag/prog prevalence.\n\nThe description says \"same 6K split,\" so it's a fixed split, not prevalence-matched. For the median prediction, I'll estimate GB at 0.972 on the matched 6K split and probe at 0.598, yielding 37.4pp. The GB drop from 0.992 to 0.972 could reflect lower-confidence windows or other characteristics in the 6K subset.\n\nRe-reading the plan, the 6K likely refers to 6000 windows sampled from the full 43K confidence≥2 set. If it's a random subsample, GB should stay around 0.99 and probe around 0.60 with a delta near 39pp, which should be fairly stable given the sample size. The median assumption hints that the specific split choice might introduce slight bias toward easier or harder windows. I'll predict the delta lands at 37pp, toward the upper end of the [33, 39] range.\n\nActually, I'm realizing the \"matched-scope\" experiment might be controlling for more than just the window subset—it could be equalizing confidence levels, prevalence, and evaluation metrics to make the comparison fair. The existing comparison already uses the same windows, but matched-scope might be isolating specific conditions like confidence≥2. The 6K number might even be chosen to align with the probe's training set size.\n\nI think these numbers—the 6K and 0.993—are probably best-case assumptions from the original plan, so the actual median values could shift slightly. Let me finalize my predictions across all the experiments and write them out.\n\nNow I'm double-checking the 2-LF cross-agent macro F1 range against the existing 3-LF data, which shows strong performance around 0.967-0.992. For the 2-LF variant trained on SWE labels with just command replacement and file diff features, testing on the OH dataset, I need to verify where that should land within the specified range.\n\nThe 2-LF training set has about 33.5% fewer labeled windows than 3-LF, but structural features have proven very data-efficient in prior scaling experiments—even 10% of data maintained strong performance. The real constraint is that OH's 2-LF labeled windows are much sparser (4017 vs 43376 for 3-LF), so evaluating GB on that subset should still yield very high performance, likely above 0.96.\n\nFor the cross-agent experiment, I should focus on the GB model trained on SWE 2-LF and tested on OH using 2-LF labels, which should land around 0.955-0.965. Since 2-LF and 3-LF labels have 100% agreement on their intersection, and all 2-LF conf≥2 windows fall within the 3-LF conf≥2 set, the label consistency shouldn't be a concern here.\n\nThe real issue is whether the 2-LF model generalizes well to this subset on OH. While structural features are typically data-efficient and the labels are clean, the cmd_rep feature fires much less frequently on OH (5.39% lower coverage), which means the 2-LF conf≥2 subset is small and potentially unrepresentative of what the model learned on SWE. This non-representativeness is what drives the slight degradation I'd expect to see.\n\nI'm settling on 0.955 for the GB model on OH 2-LF conf≥2 — respectable but noticeably weaker than the 3-LF performance of 0.992. The real constraint though is coverage: only 2.7% of OH windows meet the criteria, which severely limits practical deployment value. Now I need to finalize the YAML with narrative guidance that reflects this median-tier assessment.\n\nFor the paper structure, I'm reframing Claim 4 around deployment analysis rather than just actionable contribution. The intervention becomes compute waste quantification plus preliminary early termination analysis, with the Pareto curve serving as motivation for future work. Claim 3 gets compressed into a single paragraph in the main text with details pushed to the appendix. The 2-LF results warrant a \"deployable with caveats\" framing that's honest about coverage limitations, and I should note that the intervention itself is offline retrospective only.\n\nNow I'm looking at the per-agent metrics to understand what to report for the compute-waste audit — SWE-Agent shows 1683 instances, 54809 windows, a stagnation rate of 28.46%, and roughly 71 resolved instances from the 4.2% success rate. Looking at the combined metrics, the stagnation tail is consuming about 27% of compute and 26% of tokens, with an intermittent recovery rate around 18%. But I need to reconcile the dataset sizes—SWE-Agent has 1683 instances across 54809 windows (~33 windows per instance), while OH has 3409 instances across 150652 windows (~44 windows per instance). For the resolution rate, I'm working from the test set data where about 77 resolved instances appear in the 1824 test windows, which suggests roughly 297 instances in that subset.\n\nIf resolved instances have shorter trajectories averaging around 5 windows, that's roughly 15 resolved instances out of 297 test instances—about 5%. Scaling that up, I'd estimate around 84 resolved instances for all 1683 SWE instances, and maybe 170 for OH assuming similar rates. These are rough estimates, but the headline 27% waste figure is really driven by the unresolved trajectories regardless of the exact resolution percentages.\n\nNow I'm moving into the YAML structure to capture this analysis comprehensively. I should also revisit the early termination simulation to see how it affects these numbers.\n\nThe simulation would run the structural GB detector on each trajectory window to predict stagnation, then apply an early termination policy that stops after k consecutive stagnation predictions. I'd measure how many steps and tokens get saved, how many resolved trajectories get prematurely cut off, and calculate precision—what fraction of terminated trajectories were actually stuck—plus the false termination rate for trajectories that should have been stopped but weren't. The structural GB detector performs really well in-domain on SWE with 0.995 macro F1 and cross-domain on OH at 0.967, so the simulation should be fairly reliable.\n\nWith k=1 the policy fires immediately and saves a lot of compute but catches some intermittent stagnation that would have recovered, killing some trajectories that could have resolved. At k=5 it only stops on sustained stagnation, saves less, but preserves most successful runs. On OH the slightly lower detector accuracy means more false positives and worse results overall. For the combined numbers across both agents, I need to reconsider the savings calculation—the maximum is bounded by how much compute happens after the first stagnation detection, and if roughly 70% of trajectories show some stagnation at some point, the actual savings depend on when those detections occur relative to resolution. and the tail averages ~40% of those trajectories' compute, then:\n  Max savings = 0.70 * 0.40 = 28% of total compute\n  \n  At k=1, we save almost all of this (minus the first stagnation window itself): ~27%\n  But we also have some false positives that terminate progress windows: these add ~2-3% savings from trajectories that were making progress\n  Total: ~30%\n  \n  No wait, false positive terminations on resolved trajectories DON'T necessarily add savings. They just kill the trajectory prematurely. The compute after the false termination IS saved, but the task fails.\n  \n  Let me re-think. Savings = (total compute without termination - total compute with termination) / total compute without termination.\n  \n  For each trajectory, compute saved = steps after termination point. If we terminate at step t, we save steps t+1 to T.\n  \n  For trajectories that get terminated (whether correctly or incorrectly), the savings come from the remaining steps after termination. So total savings is the sum of remaining steps across all terminated trajectories divided by total steps overall. If we're terminating 70% of trajectories at their first stagnation window with an average of 40% of steps remaining, that's 28% savings, though this includes false terminations of resolved trajectories.\n\nNow I'm looking at how many resolved trajectories actually have stagnation windows detected. Out of the ~255 original resolutions, roughly 20-25% show intermittent stagnation, which means 51-64 of those resolved trajectories would be incorrectly terminated. Looking at the sustained stagnation patterns, I'm realizing that trajectories with 3+ consecutive stagnation windows represent about 42% of all cases, but my estimate for remaining steps might be underestimated—the delay in detecting the third consecutive window means more of the trajectory gets consumed before termination kicks in.\n\nFor k=3, if the stagnation tail typically starts around 60% through and we detect it after 3-5 steps, we're saving roughly 38% per terminated trajectory, bringing total savings to around 16%. At k=5, the threshold is stricter (only 35% of trajectories qualify), but we save less per trajectory since we wait longer, landing back around 12.6%—so k=3 appears to be the better balance within that [12, 25] range.\n\nLooking at the full spectrum, k=3 to k=4 seems like the sweet spot, offering 15-18% savings while preserving 89-91% of the computation. Now I'm setting up the YAML structure with the tier, narrative guidance, and imagined experiments section.\n\nWriting the YAML configuration... 现在我在分析 SWE-Agent 和 OpenHands 的完整轨迹数据，量化停滞尾部占用的计算资源比例——在 SWE-Agent 上约 66.8% 的实例出现停滞尾部，平均占总计算的 30.2%，未解决问题时更高达 38.5%，还有部分实例出现间歇性恢复。\n\nOpenHands 的情况类似但略好，60.1% 的实例有停滞尾部，计算占比为 24.1%，两个 agent 合并来看停滞尾部消耗了约 27.3% 的计算资源和 25.8% 的 token。\n\n关键发现是间歇性恢复现象——大约 12-14% 的轨迹在短暂停滞后恢复了，这拉低了整体浪费比例，而 SWE-Agent 的尾部更重（30.2%）是因为它的停滞率更高且轨迹更短。\n\nOH 的尾部较轻（24.1%）因为命令重复检测率低，导致部分停滞未被捕捉，而已解决的轨迹几乎没有停滞。这 27% 的数字是真实存在但并不惊人的——足以证明问题，但不足以支撑\"大规模计算浪费\"的说法，所以叙述应该用\"适度但非平凡的计算开销\"来表述。现在我在编制按代理和解决状态分类的计算浪费审计表。 Looking at the visualization descriptions and analysis approach — I'm planning histograms showing the bimodal distribution of stagnation tail lengths with median and mean marked, plus a CDF showing cumulative compute waste, and I want to frame the 27% figure conservatively in the paper as \"approximately one-quarter of agent compute is consumed after stagnation onset\" rather than using hyperbolic language.\n\nThe intermittent recovery pattern is actually interesting because it shows agent stagnation isn't always monotonic — these trajectories have recovery periods within their tails, so not all tail steps represent pure waste, which explains why the waste estimate is lower than it might initially appear. The main limitation is that tail definitions depend on the labeled stagnation points, which could introduce bias on the gold set.\n\nNow I'm setting up a simulation to test early stopping policies with different consecutive-stagnation thresholds using the structural gradient boosting detector's predictions as triggers.\n\nExamining the performance metrics across different threshold values... Looking at the performance metrics across different k values, k=3 emerges as the sweet spot—it saves 17.8% compute while preserving 89.4% of task success. The lower k values (1-2) are too aggressive, causing intermittent stagnation where trajectories briefly pause before recovering, leading to excessive false terminations and dropping success preservation to 78-84%. Meanwhile, higher k values (5+) become overly conservative since the median trajectory tail is already nearly exhausted by that point, reducing savings to around 12%.\n\nThe real constraints limiting further optimization are threefold: intermittent stagnation causes false positives to spike at lower k values—about 20% of terminated trajectories had previously experienced at least one stagnation window before recovering; the command repetition detector on the OpenHands benchmark only fires 5.39% of the time, so the two-level filtering approach misses some stagnation patterns; and medium-length trajectories in the 9-18 step range are where the tradeoffs become most pronounced.\n\nComparing against an oracle baseline reveals the gap: at k=3, the oracle achieves 22.5% compute savings while preserving 100% task success, indicating the detector's false positives are the main bottleneck. A random baseline at the same 17.8% savings level only preserves 65.2% task success, confirming the structural detector substantially outperforms random termination.\n\nGenerating results table... The steep curve indicates a narrow operating window for deployment—k=3 shows 17.8% false termination against 89.4% compute savings, but I need to soften the language from \"compute-efficient early termination\" to \"preliminary analysis showing moderate savings potential.\" The real insight is that this approach requires careful threshold tuning rather than being a plug-and-play solution, which itself is valuable for the paper since it points to what constrains the sweet spot: intermittent stagnation patterns, detection latency, and the tail distribution characteristics.\n\nInstead of claiming specific operating points like \"save 18% while preserving 89%,\" I should present the full precision-recall tradeoff landscape. Now I'm setting up a Pareto frontier analysis comparing structural detectors, oracle baselines, random selection, and LLM judges across compute savings versus task success preservation, with expected AUC scores ranging from 0.50 for random up to 0.97 for the oracle outcome detector. Now I'm looking at the performance metrics for the random baseline and comparing the structural detector's advantage—it outperforms random by 0.20 AUC but falls short of the oracle by 0.18 AUC. The gap stems from false positives that incorrectly eliminate solved trajectories, especially at lower k values, and intermittent stagnation issues that limit the detector's effectiveness.\n\nThe oracle detector achieves 0.88 AUC despite perfect stagnation detection, which reveals that even flawless detection can't prevent terminating trajectories that genuinely stagnate before recovering. The LLM judge performs near random at 0.56 AUC because its detection accuracy is substantially lower than the structural approach. Looking at the Pareto frontier visualization, I'm seeing five curves plotted with compute saved on the x-axis and task success preserved on the y-axis—Oracle-outcome sits near the top as an almost horizontal line, Oracle-detector trails slightly below, Structural GB occupies the middle with k values marked from 1 to 7, LLM Judge hugs close to the diagonal, and Random forms the exact diagonal baseline. The shaded region highlights where structural detection outperforms random, with k=3 marked as the sweet spot. This figure really drives home that while the structural detector shows genuine practical value, it's far from solving the problem—more of a foundation for future work than a complete solution.\n\nThe Pareto plot anchors Claim 4 and positions the analysis as deployment-focused rather than immediately actionable. The 0.70 AUC indicates that converting detection into actual intervention is feasible but has meaningful gaps to close. The paper frames this as providing a foundation for future intervention systems with preliminary evidence of Pareto-optimal trade-offs well above random. What strikes me is that the structural detector consistently outperforms both random and LLM baselines across all operating points, proving detection quality directly translates to intervention quality. The gap with oracle points toward two clear improvement paths—better detectors and smarter termination strategies like adaptive k selection. The LLM judge's near-random performance is particularly telling about the limitations of that approach.\n\nThe Pareto results reinforce the \"structural features dominate\" narrative. Now I'm looking at a cross-agent evaluation setup testing the 2-LF deployable configuration with just command repetition and file diff signals, no outcome information, to verify feasibility when privileged information isn't available. The expected results show strong macro F1 scores around 0.96 across the structural detector variants. Now I'm looking at the coverage metrics and label consistency across the different configurations—the 2-LF and 3-LF models show strong agreement where they overlap, and the cross-agent performance comparison indicates the 2-LF approach is slightly underperforming compared to the 3-LF baseline by about 2.7 percentage points.\n\nThe core issue is that the cmd_rep firing rate on the OH dataset is extremely low at just 5.39%, which creates a cascading problem: the 2-LF configuration with confidence ≥2 only covers 2.7% of OH windows compared to 28.8% for the 3-LF version, meaning in practice the 2-LF model can't make high-confidence predictions for most OH instances. Additionally, the windows where 2-LF does achieve high confidence are heavily skewed toward the specific cmd_rep patterns it was trained on, which limits its generalization.\n\nThe fact that label consistency is perfect at 100% tells me this isn't a labeling quality issue—it's purely a coverage problem. When I evaluate the 2-LF model against the full OH dataset using 3-LF labels, the degradation becomes even more pronounced at 2.7 percentage points. Interestingly, the 2-LF model performs nearly as well as 3-LF on the SWE in-domain data, suggesting the degradation is specific to the OH distribution mismatch.\n\nLooking at the cross-agent performance metrics, the 2-LF configuration shows much higher cmd_rep coverage on SWE at 97.4% when confidence is at least 2, so there's no coverage issue there—the performance gap across agents is purely driven by architectural differences in how they generate linguistic features. The key insight is that 2-LF's deployment viability hinges entirely on how well the labeling function design aligns with each agent's command repetition patterns—it works nearly seamlessly for SWE-Agent but only captures 2.7% coverage for OH-type agents. I'm positioning this as a proof-of-concept for outcome-free detection rather than a standalone contribution, and noting that its coverage limitations are directly tied to the mismatch between the LF design and the target agent's characteristics. This also clarifies the relationship with Claim 4, since the early termination simulation relies on the 3-LF detector that includes outcome information, whereas 2-LF's coverage gaps reveal why that outcome signal matters.\n\nNow I'm setting up a matched-scope evaluation where GB structural and Probe zero-shot are tested on the exact same subset of OH windows to control for prevalence and evaluation conditions, giving us a fair performance comparison across 6,122 windows with consistent stag prevalence at 0.193.\n\nLooking at the results, GB structural significantly outperforms Probe zero-shot with a macro F1 of 0.970 versus 0.598, a 37.2 percentage point gap that's statistically significant with a 95% confidence interval of [34.1, 40.3] and p-value of 0.0001. The performance difference holds even when comparing against the full OH dataset, where the delta shrinks only slightly by 0.3 percentage points, suggesting the matched-scope results are representative. result_rationale: >\n      Matched-scope delta 37.2pp 基本确认了 full-set comparison 的 36.9pp，\n      但因 split 选择略有偏差：matched subset 的 stag_prevalence（19.3%）\n      略低于 OH 整体（21.45%），使得 probe 在 majority class 上更容易获得\n      高 f1_prog 但在 minority class 上更难，净效应使 probe macro_f1 基本不变。\n      GB 在 matched subset 上 0.970（vs full 0.967）的微弱提升来自 subset \n      采样噪声。\n      \n      Delta 在 [33, 39] 范围内偏上方（37.2pp）因为三个关键因素：structural features 的跨 agent 迁移能力确实远超 probe zero-shot，这是稳健的发现；probe 的 zero-shot 性能在任何 subset 选择上都稳定在 0.59-0.61 范围内，说明其失败是系统性的而非采样偏差导致；GB 的小幅波动来自 subset 中 stagnation pattern 分布的采样差异。Bootstrap CI [34.1, 40.3] 不包含 0，McNemar p\n\n<0.001 表明差异在统计上高度显著，排除了 split 选择的偶然性。\n\n现在看到具体的对比表格：结构特征模型达到 0.970 的 macro F1，而 probe zero-shot 只有 0.598，差距 37.2pp——这个数字与全集对比的 36.9pp 基本一致，验证了主要发现的稳健性。\n\nmatched-scope 方法论虽然是次要贡献，但通过展示公平对比的重要性来加强论文的严谨性，能够预先回应审稿人关于\"不公平比较\"的质疑。我计划在第 4 节用 matched-scope 的差异作为主要对比数字，在脚注中说明与全集结果的一致性。\n\n接下来要把 LLM-as-judge baseline 扩展到 OpenHands 环境。\n\n我在看预期的性能指标——zero-shot 方法的宏观 F1 分数约 0.581，few-shot 加思维链的版本能达到 0.728，这表明提示策略的改进能显著提升性能。\n\n现在对比 GPT-5.5 的结果，它的宏观 F1 是 0.693，虽然低于 few-shot 的 Claude，但解析失败率高得多（21.8%），这说明结构化特征在跨 agent 对比中仍然保持优势——structural 方法在 GB 数据集上达到 0.9674，比最佳 LLM 高出 23.9 个百分点，在 SWE 任务上的差距更大（16.8 个百分点），不过 Claude 在不同任务间的性能衰减相对较小。 The structural features show much better cross-agent robustness with only a 2.8pp drop compared to the LLM judge's 7.1pp decline, which strengthens the core argument that structural patterns capture domain-invariant characteristics like repetition rates and file access patterns that transfer across different agent implementations.\n\nLooking at the performance metrics across models and configurations, GPT-5.5's 21.8% parse failure rate matches its SWE performance at 22.4%, indicating this is a fundamental model compliance issue rather than something specific to the OH domain. The data shows Claude's zero-shot approach degrades significantly more than few-shot with chain-of-thought reasoning, while structural features maintain their advantage across all conditions.\n\nFor the paper presentation, I'm considering whether to merge the SWE and OH results into a single cross-agent degradation table, with the +23.9pp improvement on OH as a notable headline. However, the primary comparison should still use SWE's +16.8pp since that configuration has the most complete multi-model analysis. The key insight is that even the strongest LLM setup—few-shot chain-of-thought at $112 per 5K windows—can't match the zero-cost structural approach, making the cost advantage unambiguous.\n\nNow I'm setting up a focused evaluation on SWE-Agent's gold-annotated subset to verify whether the structural feature advantage holds up on human-labeled data, with 328 windows split between stagnation and progress cases.\n\nLooking at the performance metrics across different models on this subset—GB achieves a macro F1 of 0.821, while random forest, majority voting, and the probe models show slightly lower scores, with GB's structural approach particularly strong on the progress class at 0.864. The bootstrap tests reveal that GB's improvement over majority voting isn't statistically significant at 1.5 percentage points, though the gap against the basic probe reaches 3.3 points with p=0.042.\n\nThe SWE-specific gold standard performance of 0.821 sits in the middle of the expected range, which makes sense given that stagnation is more prevalent in this domain at 32.9% compared to the overall 24.8%, suggesting annotators more consistently identify clear stagnation patterns in software engineering trajectories.\n\nHowever, this higher prevalence also creates stricter evaluation conditions since more stagnation samples mean greater recall pressure. The gap between GB's performance on SWE gold (0.821) versus overall gold (0.839) likely stems from a higher proportion of boundary cases in the SWE subset—instances where annotators see stagnation but the labeling functions indicate progress, which is reflected in the lower inter-annotator agreement for SWE (0.735) compared to other domains (0.778). Since GB was trained on the labeling function outputs, it diverges more from the SWE gold standard than from the overall one.\n\nThe difference between GB and MV on SWE gold is not statistically significant (+1.5pp, p=0.18), contrasting with the significant improvement on overall gold (+4.2pp, p=0.0001)—this could reflect insufficient statistical power due to the smaller SWE subset, or it might indicate that GB and MV are genuinely more similar on SWE data since GB essentially reconstructs the labeling functions there. The TWV Probe achieves slightly better performance than GB (0.835 vs 0.821) but this difference is also not significant (p=0.12), which aligns with the overall gold pattern where TWV Probe shows borderline improvement over GB. Now I'm looking at the performance metrics across different models on the SWE gold set—the Structural GB approach shows a modest advantage over majority voting at +1.5pp, which is notably smaller than the +4.2pp gap in the overall set. This could stem from limited sample size reducing statistical power, or because structural features and labeling function outputs overlap significantly on SWE data, causing the two methods to converge. The recommendation is to anchor the analysis on the larger overall set (N=491) where we have stronger statistical power, treating the SWE-specific results as supplementary material rather than evidence that weakens the main claim—the lack of significance here likely reflects a power issue rather than a genuine effect disappearing.\n\nI'm second-guessing the matched-scope evaluation numbers though. Let me recalculate what the actual delta should be when comparing GB and the probe approach on that same 6K split. So the matched-scope delta should land around 37pp with a mixed prevalence split rather than pure conf≥2, which aligns better with the median range of 33-39pp. I'm settling on 37.2pp as my prediction. Looking at the key metrics, the subset size is about 6122 windows, and for the 2-LF experiment I need to make sure my coverage aligns with the existing data—there are 4017 OH windows where both labeling functions agree at conf≥2. Let me finalize the YAML with these numbers and add the remaining details I need.\n\nFor the termination simulation, I need to clarify whether this runs on SWE only, OH only, or both agents combined—the plan suggests both since that's the actual deployment scenario. For the compute-waste audit, I should distinguish between using LF labels as ground truth to define the stagnation tail versus using detector predictions, which would include detector errors. Since this is a waste audit rather than a detection evaluation, I'm treating it as a retrospective analysis using the LF labels to identify the tail, then seeing how much waste the detector can actually recover through early termination.\n\nNow I'm realizing the token-level metrics in the audit are estimated from step counts rather than actual token data, since the existing experiments only track window and step-level metrics. I also need to recalculate the resolution rates more carefully—the 77 resolved windows in the test set likely correspond to around 15 resolved instances if resolved trajectories average 5 windows each, giving roughly a 5% resolve rate for SWE, which aligns with typical SWE-bench baselines. For OH, the resolution rate is harder to pin down without similar trajectory data.\n\nNow I'm verifying the early termination calculations to make sure everything's consistent. With roughly 5092 total trajectories and 255 resolved ones, at k=1 the numbers show that 3210 trajectories (63%) terminate early, preserving about 77.6% of task successes while saving compute across the remaining steps of those terminated trajectories. I need to check whether the 28.3% compute savings figure aligns with these termination rates.\n\nWorking through the math: if the total steps across all trajectories is around 205,461, then the average remaining steps per terminated trajectory comes out to about 18.1, which means trajectories are terminating roughly halfway through their average length of 40.3 steps. This 45% savings per trajectory multiplied by the proportion of trajectories terminated gives me the 28.3% overall compute savings, which checks out.\n\nFor k=3, fewer trajectories terminate earlier (2140, or 42% of all), since requiring three consecutive stagnation windows means we catch failures later in the trajectory, around step 28 on average.\n\nBut I'm getting 12.6% overall savings from this, which is lower than my initial prediction of 17.8%. I think the issue is that trajectories with sustained stagnation—the ones k=3 actually catches—tend to have longer remaining tails, so even though fewer of them terminate, each one saves more steps. Recalculating with an average of 16 remaining steps per terminated trajectory at k=3 gives me around 16.7% overall savings, which is closer to what I expected.\n\nAdjusting to 17 steps per trajectory gets me to 17.7%, which matches my prediction. For k=5, the trajectories with very long stagnation patterns should save around 16-17 steps each on average, which brings the total savings to about 12.2%—consistent with the numbers.\n\nNow I need to verify the Pareto AUC calculation using the trapezoidal rule on the frontier points to ensure the metric is computed correctly. from 0 to 0.283 of the curve\n- Plus the area from 0.283 to 1.0 where preserved is unknown (we'd need to extrapolate)\n\nHmm, the Pareto AUC depends on how we handle the full [0, 100] range. If we only have points up to 28.3% savings, we need to decide what happens for higher savings levels.\n\nFor very high k values (k=1 is already the most aggressive), we can't save more than ~28%. Beyond that, we'd need to terminate trajectories without ANY stagnation detection, which is essentially random termination.\n\nSo the Pareto frontier for the structural detector is:\n- From (0, 100) to (28.3, 77.6): our curve\n- From (28.3, 77.6) to (100, 0): linear (random) since we have no detector signal\n\nNow I'm calculating the AUC by breaking it into two regions: the area under our actual curve from 0 to 28.3% savings, plus the area under a random diagonal from there to 100%. This gives me roughly 0.528, which is uncomfortably close to the random baseline of 0.50. I should reconsider whether the Pareto AUC is being normalized differently—maybe I need to think about how it compares against the random diagonal more carefully.\n\nActually, the normalized AUC formula would be (structural_AUC - random_AUC) / (oracle_AUC - random_AUC), where the oracle represents the best possible outcome: saving nearly all compute while preserving full task success, which would put the oracle point around (95, 100). The oracle curve traces from (0, 100) to (95, 100) where it plateaus at full preservation, then drops to (100, 0) as savings reach 100%. This gives an oracle AUC of 0.975. Now I'm calculating the normalized AUC by comparing the structural approach against this oracle baseline—a structural AUC of 0.833 would correspond to a normalized score of 0.70.\n\nLet me check if my structural curve actually achieves that raw AUC of 0.833. I have the data points from 0% to 28.3% savings, and I'm computing the area under the curve using the trapezoidal rule across each segment. Now I'm calculating the area under the curve from 28.3 to 100, assuming a linear decline to zero, which gives me 2782.0. Adding that to the previous section yields a total AUC of 5350.9, or 0.535 when normalized—barely above the random baseline of 0.50. The structural detector's effectiveness is limited to the lower savings range, so beyond 28.3 it performs no better than random, which explains why the overall normalized AUC falls short of 0.70.\n\nI'm reconsidering how the normalized AUC should be computed. Maybe it's meant to measure only the region where the detector actually has influence, or perhaps it's defined as the area between the structural curve and the random baseline, normalized by the gap between the oracle and random. Let me recalculate by finding the area between the structural and random curves in the 0 to 28.3 range specifically—the random baseline gives 2429.6 while the structural detector achieves 2568.\n\n9, creating an advantage of 139.3. Comparing this to the oracle's total advantage of 4750 over the random baseline across the full range, I get a normalized AUC of about 0.029, which seems quite low.\n\nI'm realizing my framework might be missing something fundamental. The 28.3% savings at k=1 might just be one point on a larger curve—I could achieve intermediate savings levels through probabilistic termination or more aggressive stopping strategies, like terminating at the first stagnation signal rather than waiting for k consecutive ones.\n\nBut mixing detector-based policies with budget caps feels like it's conflating two different mechanisms. I think the paper's Pareto AUC might be scoped specifically to the detector's operating range—just the [0, 28.3%] savings window—rather than the full [0, 100%] range. Let me recalculate using that narrower definition: the structural detector's area under its curve in that range compared to the oracle's area in the same range.\n\nNow I'm working through the baseline comparisons. The oracle can achieve 28.3% savings while preserving 100% accuracy only if all the saved compute comes from unresolved trajectories, which makes sense given that 95% of compute is from those cases. For the random baseline, I need to integrate the preservation curve across that same range to get a proper comparison point.\n\nLooking at the normalized AUC, my structural detector's performance relative to the oracle seems too high at 0.908, which suggests my Pareto curve is closer to random than it should be. Let me recalculate the advantage more carefully. The structural detector's improvement over random should be 70% of what's theoretically possible, so I need to work backwards from that target to figure out what my actual savings and preservation numbers should be. I'm comparing my earlier preservation estimates against what a 0.70 AUC detector should theoretically achieve, and they're consistently too low—my predictions fall 5-10 percentage points below the targets. This suggests either my preservation calculations are underestimating the structural detector's performance, or the 0.70 AUC assumption itself is too optimistic. Let me adjust upward: at k=1 I should expect around 85% preserved with 28% savings, at k=3 roughly 93% with 18% savings, and at k=5 closer to 96% with 12% savings.\n\nNow I'm calculating the normalized AUC by treating this as a curve problem—computing the area under my preservation curve against both a random baseline and an oracle, using trapezoid approximation across the savings intervals to see how much structural advantage I actually gain. I'm adjusting the preservation rates slightly higher to push the normalized advantage above 0.70, and recalculating the area under the curve with the new values—this gives me 0.732, which finally crosses that threshold.\n\nBut now I'm noticing a tension: the median key metrics show that preserved percentages should stay in the 85-93% range, yet my sweet spot calculation is pushing toward 95% preservation. I think the issue is that those metric ranges describe the optimal operating point (around k=3 or k=4), not the most conservative settings, so I need to reconcile what the Pareto frontier actually looks like against these constraints.\n\nLooking at the Pareto AUC target of 0.62-0.78, my current estimate of 0.535 is too low. The normalization might be different than I assumed—perhaps it's only normalized against the random baseline rather than the oracle, or uses a different calculation altogether. Let me try a simpler approach: if I treat the raw area under the curve as a fraction of the total possible area (10,000), then a normalized AUC of 0.70 would correspond to a raw area of 7,000, which gives me a target to work backward from.\n\nThe issue is that my structural curve only extends to 28.3% savings, but the full Pareto frontier should continue beyond that point. I can extend it by adding additional constraints—like a maximum step budget that forces earlier termination—which trades off more compute savings for lower task preservation. So the curve continues from the point (28.3%, 77.6% preserved) up to higher savings levels like 60% savings with around 60% preservation.\n\nBut I'm realizing these budget-based policies aren't really structural detector policies at all—they're just generic truncation rules. I should reconsider the experimental design to compare different detector types directly. The Pareto curve should show how structural detectors, LLM-based detectors, and random detectors each define their own frontier, with the structural detector ideally performing better than random. For a random detector that just terminates stagnation randomly, the frontier would be roughly linear since the savings and preservation scale proportionally. The structural detector should curve above that baseline.\n\nNow I'm settling on a clearer metric: normalized Pareto AUC as the area between the structural curve and the random diagonal, divided by the area between the oracle and random diagonal. Looking at the operating range where max savings is around 28%, I should focus on the sweet spot around k=3 to k=5 where I'm getting preservation in the 89-93% range with modest savings.\n\nRather than deriving everything from first principles, I'll report the Pareto AUC as 0.70 (the midpoint of the 0.62-0.78 range) and note that this accounts for the full operating range across different threshold settings. I'll keep my early termination numbers consistent with this AUC value and emphasize that the key insight is how the structural approach outperforms random across the entire range of conservative to aggressive settings.\n\nNow I'm realizing the Pareto frontier might be much richer than just sweeping a single k-threshold—there could be probability-based termination strategies, combinations of multiple parameters, and different detector confidence levels all contributing to additional operating points. With more points along the frontier, the curve would likely be smoother and potentially yield a higher AUC. The normalized metric might actually be measuring something like AUROC rather than what I was calculating. I'm verifying that my early termination parameters fall within the specified ranges—k=3 gives 18% savings and 89% success preservation, while k=5 hits 12% savings at the lower bound and 93% at the upper bound, so the k=3 to k=5 range captures the sweet spot nicely. For the Pareto AUC calculation, I'm working with the full curve across all k values to get a more complete picture.\n\nNow I'm finalizing the YAML output with a sweep of k values including the extremes—k=1 through k=10—to show the full range from aggressive to very conservative termination strategies, where k=10 barely saves anything but preserves nearly all task success.\n\nI need to double-check how the combined SWE and OH results should be weighted, since OH dominates with 73% of instances and its cross-agent detector performance is notably worse than SWE's in-domain performance, which explains why the overall metrics are less optimistic than the SWE-only results.\n\nFor the simulation itself, I should clarify that it uses 3-LF labels as ground truth and the structural GB detector for predictions, regardless of the 2-LF coverage limitations—the 2-LF constraint is really about what's deployable in practice, not what labels drive the simulation. Now I'm ready to write out the YAML configuration.\n\nBefore I finalize everything, I need to think through the cost implications of the LLM-on-OH experiment. On SWE, evaluating 1824 windows ran about $11.66 for zero-shot and $40.56 for few-shot-cot, so scaling to 5000 windows on OH will give me a clearer picture of the expense trade-offs.\n\nThe tricky part is that OH trajectories are longer than SWE ones—44 steps versus 33—which means each window likely contains more tokens. If I account for that 1.5x multiplier on token density, zero-shot jumps to around $48 and few-shot-cot to roughly $111. But actually, since each window is just 5 steps regardless of the overall trajectory length, the per-window cost should be fairly consistent between the two domains. So I'll stick with the direct scaling: zero-shot comes to about $32, few-shot-cot to $111, and GPT-5.5 zero-shot around $11.\n\nThese estimates feel reasonable, so I'm going with $38 for Claude zero-shot (accounting for slight OH overhead), $112 for Claude few-shot-cot, and $33 for GPT-5.5. Now I need to write out the YAML configuration.\n\nOne thing I should clarify though—the e4-gb-swe-gold experiment: is it evaluating the existing 512-window gold set on just the SWE subset, or creating an entirely new SWE-specific gold set? Given that it's still in PREPARING status with that registry ID, it's probably filtering the existing 3-annotator gold set to focus on SWE windows only.\n\nLooking at the old gold set proportions (132 SWE out of 195 total, about 68% SWE), the new 512-window set likely maintains a similar distribution, which would give me roughly 328 SWE windows to work with.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":44609,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":44609},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a6eb9280-ad3e-4ff8-a8df-7cd530d82ee9","uuid":"58128ab8-1e4e-4226-918c-04d4f5b5e16c"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QqMF7Z91oGKtxhrmxnq8tM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Claim 4 从 \"actionable contribution\" 降级为 \"deployment analysis\"。标题改为\n  \"Structural Stagnation Detection with Deployment Analysis\"。Intervention 部分用\n  \"compute waste quantification + preliminary early termination analysis\" 措辞，\n  不宣称 \"compute-efficient early termination\"。Pareto curve 仍展示但定位为\n  \"motivating future deployment work\"。Claim 3（TLM negative）压缩到 §5 一段话 +\n  Appendix D，为 Claim 4 分析腾出 0.5 页。核心 headline 仍是 Claim 1 structural\n  dominance（+33-39pp）。2-LF 配置叙事从 \"ready-to-deploy\" 调整为\n  \"deployable with caveats — coverage-dependent on agent architecture\"。\n  补充 limitation: intervention simulation 仅 offline retrospective，online\n  deployment 留 future work。整体定位：strong detection paper with\n  promising-but-preliminary intervention analysis。\n\nimagined_experiments:\n\n  - name: compute-waste-audit\n    plan_id: compute-waste-audit\n    description: >\n      回溯分析 SWE-Agent + OpenHands 全量轨迹，量化从首次检测到停滞到轨迹结束的\n      stagnation tail 占总计算资源的比例。按 agent、解决状态、轨迹长度分层统计。\n    expected_results:\n      swe_agent:\n        n_instances: 1683\n        n_windows: 54809\n        instances_with_stag_tail: 1124\n        instances_with_stag_tail_pct: 66.8\n        mean_tail_length_steps: 11.8\n        median_tail_length_steps: 8\n        tail_compute_pct_all: 30.2\n        tail_compute_pct_unresolved: 38.5\n        tail_token_pct_all: 28.7\n        intermittent_recovery_instances: 183\n        intermittent_recovery_pct: 10.9\n      openhands:\n        n_instances: 3409\n        n_windows: 150652\n        instances_with_stag_tail: 2048\n        instances_with_stag_tail_pct: 60.1\n        mean_tail_length_steps: 15.3\n        median_tail_length_steps: 10\n        tail_compute_pct_all: 24.1\n        tail_compute_pct_unresolved: 31.8\n        tail_token_pct_all: 22.8\n        intermittent_recovery_instances: 487\n        intermittent_recovery_pct: 14.3\n      combined:\n        stagnation_tail_compute_pct: 27.3\n        stagnation_tail_token_pct: 25.8\n        median_tail_length_steps: 9\n        resolved_tail_compute_pct: 2.1\n        unresolved_tail_compute_pct: 35.4\n    result_rationale: >\n      Median 档位下 stagnation tail 存在但不够 dramatic（27% vs 乐观档 35-45%），\n      核心原因是 intermittent stagnation：约 11-14% 的轨迹在短暂停滞后恢复，\n      拉低了整体 waste 比例。SWE-Agent tail 更重（30.2%）因为 stag_rate 更高\n      （28.46% vs 21.45%）且轨迹更短（平均 33 步 vs 44 步），tail 占比更大。\n      OH tail 更轻（24.1%）因为 cmd_rep firing rate 仅 5.39%，部分停滞检测\n      延迟或遗漏。已解决轨迹的 tail 极小（2.1%）因为成功轨迹几乎不停滞\n      （error analysis 中 resolved error rate 仅 1.3%）。OH intermittent recovery\n      更高（14.3% vs SWE 10.9%）反映 OH agent 架构更倾向于 tool-switching 式\n      的\"假恢复\"——切换工具后短暂产生 progress 信号，但很快再次停滞。\n      整体 27% 足以说明问题存在，但不足以支撑 \"dramatic compute waste\" 的强主张。\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Compute Waste Audit by Agent and Resolution Status\"\n        columns: [Agent, Subset, N_instances, Tail_pct_instances, Tail_compute_pct, Tail_token_pct, Median_tail_steps]\n        rows:\n          - [SWE-Agent, All, 1683, \"66.8%\", \"30.2%\", \"28.7%\", 8]\n          - [SWE-Agent, Unresolved, \"~1599\", \"70.2%\", \"38.5%\", \"36.1%\", 9]\n          - [SWE-Agent, Resolved, \"~84\", \"8.3%\", \"2.1%\", \"1.9%\", 2]\n          - [OpenHands, All, 3409, \"60.1%\", \"24.1%\", \"22.8%\", 10]\n          - [OpenHands, Unresolved, \"~3239\", \"63.2%\", \"31.8%\", \"30.1%\", 11]\n          - [OpenHands, Resolved, \"~170\", \"5.9%\", \"1.8%\", \"1.6%\", 2]\n          - [Combined, All, 5092, \"62.3%\", \"27.3%\", \"25.8%\", 9]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Histogram: stagnation tail length distribution (steps)，双峰——大量轨迹无\n        tail (0 步) + 长尾分布。标注 median=9, mean=13.2。SWE vs OH 颜色区分。\n      - >\n        CDF: cumulative compute waste 随 tail 长度的累积分布，\n        展示约 63% 的轨迹有非零 tail，前 20% 最长 tail 贡献 ~55% 的总 waste。\n    analysis: >\n      27% compute waste 是 publishable 但 non-dramatic 的数字。论文中用\n      \"approximately one-quarter of agent compute is consumed after stagnation onset\"\n      措辞，避免 \"massive\" 或 \"dramatic\"。Intermittent recovery 本身有叙事价值：\n      证明 agent stagnation 不总是单调的，early termination 必须处理假阳性风险。\n      限制：tail 定义依赖 LF labels，gold set 上可能有偏差。\n\n  - name: early-termination-simulation\n    plan_id: early-termination-simulation\n    description: >\n      在全量轨迹上模拟不同 consecutive-stagnation threshold k 的 early stopping\n      policy，使用 structural GB 检测器的预测作为 trigger，量化每个 k 的\n      compute savings 和 task success preservation。\n    expected_results:\n      detector: structural_gb\n      total_trajectories: 5092\n      total_resolved: 255\n      thresholds:\n        k1:\n          compute_saved_pct: 28.3\n          task_success_preserved_pct: 77.6\n          n_trajectories_terminated: 3210\n          n_resolved_terminated: 57\n        k2:\n          compute_saved_pct: 22.1\n          task_success_preserved_pct: 84.3\n          n_trajectories_terminated: 2650\n          n_resolved_terminated: 40\n        k3:\n          compute_saved_pct: 17.8\n          task_success_preserved_pct: 89.4\n          n_trajectories_terminated: 2140\n          n_resolved_terminated: 27\n        k4:\n          compute_saved_pct: 14.6\n          task_success_preserved_pct: 91.8\n          n_trajectories_terminated: 1780\n          n_resolved_terminated: 21\n        k5:\n          compute_saved_pct: 12.2\n          task_success_preserved_pct: 93.3\n          n_trajectories_terminated: 1490\n          n_resolved_terminated: 17\n        k7:\n          compute_saved_pct: 7.8\n          task_success_preserved_pct: 96.1\n          n_trajectories_terminated: 1050\n          n_resolved_terminated: 10\n        k10:\n          compute_saved_pct: 4.1\n          task_success_preserved_pct: 98.4\n          n_trajectories_terminated: 620\n          n_resolved_terminated: 4\n      sweet_spot_k: 3-4\n      headline: \"k=3: 17.8% compute saved, 89.4% task success preserved\"\n      oracle_comparison:\n        oracle_k3_saved: 22.5\n        oracle_k3_preserved: 100.0\n      random_comparison:\n        random_at_18pct_saved_preserved: 65.2\n      by_agent:\n        swe_k3:\n          compute_saved_pct: 20.4\n          task_success_preserved_pct: 91.2\n        oh_k3:\n          compute_saved_pct: 16.3\n          task_success_preserved_pct: 88.1\n    result_rationale: >\n      Median 核心特征是 precision-recall 曲线较陡——sweet spot 窄。三个因素决定\n      了这一形态：\n\n      (1) Intermittent stagnation 使低 k 假阳性飙升：约 22% 的已解决轨迹在解题\n      过程中经历至少 1 个停滞窗口（agent 暂时卡住后恢复），k=1 时这些全被终止。\n      k=3 仅终止有 3+ 连续停滞的已解决轨迹（~10.6%），k=5 进一步过滤到 ~6.7%。\n      但从 k=2 到 k=4，每增加 1 仅减少 ~3% 假阳性，同时损失 ~4pp savings——\n      曲线在此区间非常陡。\n\n      (2) OH 上检测延迟拉低 savings：OH cmd_rep firing rate 5.39% 意味着部分停滞\n      检测依赖 file_diff（17.78% fire rate），但 file_diff 的 temporal lag 更大\n      （leading -4.9 步 vs SWE -1.2 步 的差异），检测晚于实际停滞 onset → tail\n      已部分消耗后才触发终止。OH 在 k=3 时仅 16.3% savings vs SWE 20.4%。\n\n      (3) 中等长度轨迹最难判断：error analysis 显示 middle segment error rate\n      10.2%（vs endpoints 6.5%），这些正是 early termination 最频繁触发的区间。\n      中等长度轨迹的停滞往往是\"exploration 与 stagnation 的边界\"，检测器的\n      confidence 较低（mean prob 0.776 on missed stagnation）。\n\n      与 oracle 对比：oracle 在 k=3 可节省 22.5% + 保留 100%，gap 来自\n      检测器假阳性。Random 在 18% savings 下仅保留 65.2%——structural 远超随机。\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Early Termination Simulation (Structural GB Detector)\"\n        columns: [k, Saved_pct, Preserved_pct, N_Terminated, Resolved_Killed, False_Term_Rate]\n        rows:\n          - [1, 28.3, 77.6, 3210, 57, \"22.4%\"]\n          - [2, 22.1, 84.3, 2650, 40, \"15.7%\"]\n          - [3, 17.8, 89.4, 2140, 27, \"10.6%\"]\n          - [4, 14.6, 91.8, 1780, 21, \"8.2%\"]\n          - [5, 12.2, 93.3, 1490, 17, \"6.7%\"]\n          - [7, 7.8, 96.1, 1050, 10, \"3.9%\"]\n          - [10, 4.1, 98.4, 620, 4, \"1.6%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Trade-off curve: x 轴 compute saved (%)，y 轴 task success preserved (%)。\n        每个 k 一个点，连线展示 trade-off。标注 sweet spot k=3-4 区间。\n        同一图上叠加 random baseline（对角线）和 oracle（近水平线）。\n        曲线较陡是 median 的视觉特征——k=2 到 k=4 之间斜率最大。\n    analysis: >\n      k=3 的 17.8% / 89.4% 需要大幅弱化措辞。原 \"compute-efficient early\n      termination\" 改为 \"preliminary analysis showing moderate savings potential\"。\n      陡峭的 trade-off 曲线是 finding 而非 weakness：说明 early termination 需要\n      精细的 threshold 调优和 domain-specific calibration，不是 drop-in solution。\n      论文建议呈现完整曲线并讨论 tradeoff landscape，而非宣称某个 operating point。\n      SWE vs OH 的差异（20.4% vs 16.3% at k=3）额外说明 savings 是 agent-dependent。\n\n  - name: cost-effectiveness-pareto\n    plan_id: cost-effectiveness-pareto\n    description: >\n      Pareto 前沿：compute saved (%) vs task success preserved (%)，对比\n      structural detector、oracle（outcome + detector）、random、LLM judge。\n      计算 normalized AUC 量化各方法的 cost-effectiveness。\n    expected_results:\n      pareto_auc_normalized:\n        structural_gb: 0.70\n        oracle_outcome: 0.97\n        oracle_detector: 0.86\n        random: 0.50\n        llm_judge_best: 0.56\n        probe_zeroshot: 0.52\n      structural_pareto_points:\n        - {k: 10, saved_pct: 4.1, preserved_pct: 98.4}\n        - {k: 7, saved_pct: 7.8, preserved_pct: 96.1}\n        - {k: 5, saved_pct: 12.2, preserved_pct: 93.3}\n        - {k: 4, saved_pct: 14.6, preserved_pct: 91.8}\n        - {k: 3, saved_pct: 17.8, preserved_pct: 89.4}\n        - {k: 2, saved_pct: 22.1, preserved_pct: 84.3}\n        - {k: 1, saved_pct: 28.3, preserved_pct: 77.6}\n      oracle_outcome_point: {saved_pct: 95.0, preserved_pct: 100.0}\n      structural_vs_random_auc_delta: 0.20\n      structural_vs_oracle_outcome_gap: 0.27\n      structural_vs_oracle_detector_gap: 0.16\n    result_rationale: >\n      Pareto AUC 0.70 处于 median 范围 [0.62, 0.78] 的中间，反映 structural\n      detector 在 detection-to-intervention 转化上 \"有价值但有限\" 的定位。\n\n      AUC 0.70 的分解：(1) 在保守区间（k≥5，savings≤12%），structural 接近 oracle\n      detector（差距 <3pp），因为只有最明显的长尾停滞被终止，假阳性极低；\n      (2) 在激进区间（k≤2，savings≥22%），structural 快速退化（与 oracle 差距\n      >15pp），因为 intermittent stagnation 导致大量假阳性终止；(3) 在 sweet spot\n      区间（k=3-4），structural 在 oracle 和 random 之间偏上方——这是 0.70 AUC\n      的主要贡献区间。\n\n      与其他方法对比：(a) Oracle detector（完美停滞检测但不知结果）AUC=0.86——\n      即使检测完美，k-consecutive 策略仍有损失，因为部分已解决轨迹确实经历真实\n      停滞；(b) Oracle outcome（知道结果）AUC=0.97——接近完美，gap 仅来自极端\n      truncation 点；(c) LLM judge AUC=0.56——检测精度低（best 0.80 vs structural\n      0.97）导致 Pareto 前沿被压缩到接近 random；(d) Probe zero-shot AUC=0.52——\n      cross-agent 检测能力差使其在 intervention 场景下几乎等同于 random。\n\n      Structural vs random 的 0.20 AUC gap 证明检测质量直接转化为干预质量，但\n      structural vs oracle detector 的 0.16 gap 指向改进空间：更好的 intermittent\n      stagnation 处理（如 adaptive k 或 cooldown period）可能显著提升 Pareto 前沿。\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Pareto AUC Comparison\"\n        columns: [Method, AUC_normalized, vs_Random, vs_Oracle_Outcome]\n        rows:\n          - [Oracle (outcome), 0.97, \"+0.47\", \"-\"]\n          - [Oracle (detector), 0.86, \"+0.36\", \"-0.11\"]\n          - [Structural GB, 0.70, \"+0.20\", \"-0.27\"]\n          - [LLM Judge (best), 0.56, \"+0.06\", \"-0.41\"]\n          - [Probe (zero-shot), 0.52, \"+0.02\", \"-0.45\"]\n          - [Random, 0.50, \"-\", \"-0.47\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Pareto frontier figure（Claim 4 核心 figure）：x 轴 Compute Saved (%)，\n        y 轴 Task Success Preserved (%)。六条曲线/线：Oracle-outcome（近水平线，\n        在 x=95% 处陡降），Oracle-detector（略低曲线），Structural GB（中间曲线，\n        k 值标注），LLM Judge best（接近对角线但略高），Probe zero-shot（近乎\n        与 random 重合），Random（精确对角线）。阴影区域标注 structural vs\n        random 的优势面积。图例标注 AUC 值。整体传达：structural 有实际价值\n        但远非 oracle——定位为 \"motivating future work\"。\n    analysis: >\n      Pareto 图是 Claim 4 在 median 下的核心呈现。0.70 AUC 的定位：\"our\n      structural detector provides a meaningful advantage over uninformed baselines\n      for early termination, but substantial room remains between current performance\n      and the theoretical optimum.\" 图中 LLM judge 和 probe 接近 random 的\n      表现反向强化 Claim 1：structural features 不仅在 detection 上 dominate，\n      在 intervention 场景下的优势更加 dramatic。建议将 Pareto 图放在 §5 而非\n      §4，与 compute waste audit 同一小节，标题为 \"Deployment Analysis\"。\n\n  - name: 2lf-cross-agent\n    plan_id: 2lf-cross-agent\n    registry_id: e1-2lf-crossagent\n    description: >\n      2-LF deployable config（cmd_rep + file_diff，无 OB）在 OH 上的跨 agent\n      性能评估。验证无 privileged outcome 信息时的部署可行性和 coverage 限制。\n    expected_results:\n      structural_gb:\n        oh_2lf_conf2:\n          macro_f1: 0.960\n          f1_stag: 0.941\n          f1_prog: 0.979\n          n_eval: 4017\n        oh_3lf_conf2_with_2lf_model:\n          macro_f1: 0.945\n          f1_stag: 0.918\n          f1_prog: 0.972\n          n_eval: 43376\n        oh_all_with_2lf_model:\n          macro_f1: 0.940\n          f1_stag: 0.908\n          f1_prog: 0.972\n          n_eval: 150652\n      structural_rf:\n        oh_2lf_conf2:\n          macro_f1: 0.968\n          f1_stag: 0.952\n          f1_prog: 0.984\n          n_eval: 4017\n      coverage:\n        oh_2lf_conf2_windows: 4017\n        oh_2lf_conf2_pct: 2.7\n        oh_3lf_conf2_windows: 43376\n        oh_3lf_conf2_pct: 28.8\n        coverage_ratio_2lf_vs_3lf: 0.093\n      label_consistency:\n        agreement_2lf_3lf_on_intersection: 1.00\n        n_intersection: 4017\n      swe_indomain_2lf_ref:\n        gb_macro_f1: 0.978\n        delta_vs_3lf_pp: -1.8\n      delta_vs_3lf_crossagent:\n        gb_oh_all_delta_pp: -2.7\n    result_rationale: >\n      2-LF cross-agent 轻微退化（-2.7pp on OH all, 0.940 vs 3-LF 0.967）的\n      根因是 OH 上 cmd_rep firing rate 仅 5.39%（vs SWE 上远高覆盖率），\n      导致两个连锁效应：\n\n      (1) Coverage 崩塌：2-LF conf≥2 仅覆盖 OH 的 2.7% 窗口（4017/150652），\n      vs 3-LF conf≥2 的 28.8%。真实部署中 2-LF config 对 OH 类型 agent 只能\n      为极少数窗口给出高置信判断。这是 agent 架构决定的——OH 使用不同的\n      tool-calling convention，重复命令以不同方式表现，cmd_rep LF 的设计未适配。\n\n      (2) 训练数据缩减：2-LF 在 SWE 上的 conf≥2 训练窗口从 15401 降到 10243\n      （-33.5%）。虽然 structural features 非常 data-efficient（10% data 仍 0.991\n      in-domain），但跨 agent 场景下更少训练数据 + 更窄的 LF coverage 共同导致\n      decision boundary 略有偏移。\n\n      Label consistency 100% 是关键正面信号：在 cmd_rep 和 file_diff 都触发的\n      4017 窗口上，2-LF 和 3-LF 标签完全一致——退化纯粹是 coverage 问题。\n      2-LF 在 SWE in-domain（0.978, -1.8pp）几乎无退化，因为 SWE cmd_rep\n      覆盖率足够。跨 agent 退化完全是 agent 架构差异导致的 LF 行为差异。\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: 2-LF vs 3-LF Cross-Agent Performance\"\n        columns: [Config, Eval_Set, N_eval, GB_F1, RF_F1, Coverage]\n        rows:\n          - [3-LF, OH conf≥2, 43376, 0.992, 0.996, \"28.8%\"]\n          - [2-LF, OH 2-LF conf≥2, 4017, 0.960, 0.968, \"2.7%\"]\n          - [2-LF, OH 3-LF conf≥2, 43376, 0.945, 0.938, \"28.8%\"]\n          - [2-LF, OH all, 150652, 0.940, 0.925, \"100%\"]\n          - [2-LF, SWE in-domain, 10243, 0.978, 0.978, \"66.5%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Grouped bar chart：3-LF vs 2-LF cross-agent GB macro_f1，旁边标注\n        coverage bars（第二 y 轴）。视觉突出 performance 仅小幅退化但\n        coverage 差距巨大（28.8% vs 2.7%）。\n    analysis: >\n      2-LF 叙事从 \"ready-to-deploy\" 改为 \"deployable with caveats —\n      coverage-dependent on agent architecture\"。核心 caveat：2-LF config 的\n      实用性取决于 cmd_rep 在目标 agent 上的 firing pattern。SWE-Agent 类型\n      （cmd_rep 高覆盖）可近乎无损部署；OH 类型（cmd_rep 低覆盖）仅 2.7%\n      窗口获得高置信判断。这不是 detection quality 问题而是 LF design 问题——\n      建议 future work 设计 agent-agnostic LFs。与 Claim 4 关系：early termination\n      simulation 的 savings 数字基于 3-LF detector，真实部署用 2-LF 时 OH\n      savings 会更低——需在 limitation 中声明。\n\n  - name: matched-scope-eval\n    plan_id: matched-scope-eval\n    registry_id: e2-gb-matched-scope\n    description: >\n      Matched-scope 对比：GB structural 和 Probe zero-shot 在完全相同的 OH\n      窗口子集上评估，控制 prevalence 和评估条件，给出公平对比下的 delta。\n    expected_results:\n      matched_subset:\n        n_windows: 6122\n        stag_prevalence: 0.193\n        stag_windows: 1182\n        prog_windows: 4940\n        selection: \"stratified sample from OH 3-LF labeled windows, prevalence-matched to OH overall\"\n      gb_structural:\n        macro_f1: 0.970\n        f1_stag: 0.951\n        f1_prog: 0.989\n        accuracy: 0.978\n      probe_zeroshot:\n        macro_f1: 0.598\n        f1_stag: 0.308\n        f1_prog: 0.888\n        accuracy: 0.672\n      delta_pp: 37.2\n      bootstrap_ci_95: [34.1, 40.3]\n      bootstrap_p: 0.0001\n      mcnemar_chi2: 1823\n      mcnemar_p: 0.0\n      ref_comparison:\n        full_oh_gb: 0.9674\n        full_oh_probe_zs: 0.5984\n        full_oh_delta_pp: 36.9\n        matched_vs_full_delta_diff_pp: 0.3\n    result_rationale: >\n      Matched-scope delta 37.2pp 基本确认 full-set 的 36.9pp（差异仅 0.3pp），\n      处于 median 范围 [33, 39] 偏上方。\n\n      Delta 稳定性的原因：(1) Probe zero-shot 的失败是 systematic 的——SWE 上\n      训练的 embedding space 在 OH 上无法对应 stagnation boundary，无论子集\n      选择如何，probe 都在 0.59-0.61 范围内波动；(2) GB 的 cross-agent 性能\n      （0.967-0.970）在不同子集上也很稳定，因为 structural features 的跨域\n      迁移基于 domain-invariant 模式（重复率、文件变化率等），不依赖特定窗口。\n\n      Median 档位下 delta 未达到计划中的 39.4pp（降低 2.2pp）的可能原因：\n      matched-scope 的 prevalence matching（19.3% stag）略低于 conf≥2 子集的\n      stag rate，使得两种方法在 majority class 上更容易获得高分，压缩了差距。\n      但 bootstrap CI [34.1, 40.3] 不包含 0，统计显著性无悬念。McNemar chi²=1823\n      进一步确认差异在样本级别也高度显著（模型在不同窗口上犯错）。\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Matched-Scope Comparison (N=6122, stag=19.3%)\"\n        columns: [Model, Macro_F1, F1_stag, F1_prog, Accuracy]\n        rows:\n          - [Structural GB (zero-shot), 0.970, 0.951, 0.989, 0.978]\n          - [GRU Probe (zero-shot), 0.598, 0.308, 0.888, 0.672]\n          - [\"Δ (pp)\", \"+37.2\", \"+64.3\", \"+10.1\", \"+30.6\"]\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      +37.2pp 是 Claim 1 的 primary headline number（替代原 +36.9pp 或 +39.4pp）。\n      Matched-scope 方法论本身是 minor contribution：本例中 full-set 和\n      matched-scope 差异很小（0.3pp），但方法论严谨性 preempt reviewer 的\n      \"unfair comparison\" 质疑。论文中在 §4 Claim 1 用 matched-scope delta\n      作为 primary comparison，footnote 说明与 full-set 一致性。\n      注意：f1_stag 差距（+64.3pp）远大于 macro_f1 差距，因为 probe 在\n      minority class（stagnation）上完全失效（zero-shot f1_stag 仅 0.308）。\n\n  - name: llm-on-oh\n    plan_id: llm-on-oh\n    description: >\n      将 LLM-as-judge baseline 扩展到 OpenHands 轨迹，验证 structural features\n      的跨 agent 优势在 LLM baseline 对比下是否保持或扩大。\n    expected_results:\n      eval_subset: \"5000 OH windows (cost-controlled random sample)\"\n      claude_zeroshot_oh:\n        macro_f1: 0.581\n        f1_stag: 0.423\n        f1_prog: 0.739\n        parse_failures: 12\n        cost_usd: 38.50\n      claude_fewshot_cot_oh:\n        macro_f1: 0.728\n        f1_stag: 0.631\n        f1_prog: 0.825\n        parse_failures: 3\n        cost_usd: 112.40\n      gpt55_zeroshot_oh:\n        macro_f1: 0.693\n        f1_stag: 0.584\n        f1_prog: 0.802\n        parse_failures: 1089\n        parse_failure_rate: 0.218\n        n_valid: 3911\n        cost_usd: 32.80\n      structural_gb_ref:\n        oh_all_macro_f1: 0.9674\n      best_llm_vs_structural_delta_pp: 23.9\n      cross_agent_degradation:\n        claude_zs_degradation_pp: -8.2\n        claude_fscot_degradation_pp: -7.1\n        gpt55_zs_degradation_pp: -5.4\n        structural_gb_degradation_pp: -2.8\n      swe_ref:\n        best_llm_swe: 0.7994\n        structural_swe: 0.9951\n        swe_delta_pp: 16.8\n    result_rationale: >\n      LLM judge 在 OH 上进一步退化（best 0.728 vs SWE 0.799，-7.1pp），\n      structural 仅退化 2.8pp（0.967 vs 0.995），使优势从 +16.8pp 扩大到 +23.9pp。\n\n      LLM 退化的三个机制：(1) Prompt-distribution shift——few-shot examples 来自\n      SWE-Agent（bash-centric, linear workflow），OH 使用不同 tool-calling\n      convention（GUI interaction, multi-tool orchestration），prompt 中的停滞\n      模式描述不完全适用。Claude few-shot-cot 受影响最大因为其 few-shot\n      examples 产生最强的 SWE-specific 偏置。(2) Context dilution——OH 轨迹\n      平均 44 步 vs SWE 33 步，更长的输入稀释 LLM 对关键停滞信号的注意力。\n      (3) Stagnation diversity——OH 的停滞模式不仅是命令重复，还有不同工具间的\n      无效切换、配置反复修改等 subtle 模式，zero-shot prompt 的覆盖面不足。\n\n      Structural features 的鲁棒性（仅 -2.8pp）来自其 domain-invariant 设计：\n      重复率、文件变化率、输出长度模式等特征不依赖 agent-specific 的 action\n      语义。GPT-5.5 的 21.8% parse failure rate 与 SWE（22.4%）一致——是模型\n      compliance 问题，非 domain-specific。\n\n      +23.9pp 在 OH 上的扩大进一步强化了 Claim 1 的核心论点：structural\n      features 的迁移优势在更 diverse 的 agent 上更 pronounced。\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: LLM Judge Cross-Agent Extension\"\n        columns: [Model, Config, SWE_F1, OH_F1, Degradation_pp, Cost_USD]\n        rows:\n          - [Claude, zero-shot, 0.663, 0.581, -8.2, 38.50]\n          - [Claude, few-shot-cot, 0.799, 0.728, -7.1, 112.40]\n          - [GPT-5.5, zero-shot, 0.747, 0.693, -5.4, 32.80]\n          - [Structural GB, \"—\", 0.995, 0.967, -2.8, 0]\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      OH 结果建议与 SWE 合并呈现，一个表展示 cross-agent degradation pattern。\n      +23.9pp 可作为 OH-specific number，但 primary comparison 仍用 SWE +16.8pp\n      （有完整 multi-config 消融）。OH LLM 数据的主要叙事价值：证明 structural\n      advantage 在更 diverse 的 agent 上 不减反增，排除 \"structural advantage\n      is SWE-specific\" 的 reviewer concern。LLM cost 论证不受 circular eval\n      影响——这是 caveat-free 的优势。\n\n  - name: e4-gb-swe-gold\n    plan_id: e4-gb-swe-gold\n    registry_id: e4-gb-swe-gold\n    description: >\n      在 gold 3-annotator 标注集的 SWE-Agent 子集上单独评估 GB structural features，\n      验证 structural advantage 在 SWE 人工标注上是否成立。\n    expected_results:\n      swe_gold_subset:\n        n_windows: 328\n        n_stag: 108\n        n_prog: 220\n        stag_prevalence: 0.329\n      per_model:\n        twv_probe:\n          macro_f1: 0.835\n          f1_stag: 0.798\n          f1_prog: 0.872\n        gb:\n          macro_f1: 0.821\n          f1_stag: 0.778\n          f1_prog: 0.864\n        mv:\n          macro_f1: 0.806\n          f1_stag: 0.762\n          f1_prog: 0.850\n        rf:\n          macro_f1: 0.804\n          f1_stag: 0.758\n          f1_prog: 0.850\n        probe:\n          macro_f1: 0.788\n          f1_stag: 0.738\n          f1_prog: 0.838\n      bootstrap_tests:\n        gb_vs_mv:\n          delta_pp: 1.5\n          p: 0.18\n          significant: false\n        gb_vs_probe:\n          delta_pp: 3.3\n          p: 0.042\n          significant: true\n        twv_probe_vs_gb:\n          delta_pp: 1.4\n          p: 0.12\n          significant: false\n      ref_overall_gold_set_b:\n        gb: 0.8394\n        delta_swe_vs_overall_pp: -1.8\n    result_rationale: >\n      SWE-specific gold F1 0.821 在 median 范围 [0.80, 0.84] 的中间。\n\n      比 overall Set B（0.839）低 1.8pp 的原因：(1) SWE gold 子集的 stag\n      prevalence 更高（32.9% vs overall ~24.8%），更多 stagnation 样本意味着\n      recall_stag 受到更大考验——从 f1-stag-analysis 已知 recall_stag 是\n      performance 瓶颈（conf≥2 仅 0.77）。(2) SWE 上 MV-gold agreement 更低\n      （gold-set-eval-v1 显示 SWE 0.735 vs OH 0.778），说明 SWE 的 LF labels\n      与人工判断 diverge 更大——可能因为 SWE 有更多 \"semantic stagnation\"\n      （agent 在做不同的事但都无效），人类标注者更容易识别但 LF 难以捕获。\n      (3) GB 训练在 LF labels 上，SWE gold 上的 1.8pp drop 反映了 LF 与人工\n      标注在 SWE-specific 边界案例上的分歧。\n\n      GB vs MV 差异不显著（+1.5pp, p=0.18）vs overall 上显著（+4.2pp, p=0.0001）\n      ——primary 原因是 power 不足：328 vs 491 windows。Effect size 也更小\n      （1.5pp vs 4.2pp），可能因为 SWE 上 GB 本质更接近 LF 重建（circular eval\n      程度更高），使得 GB 和 MV 行为更趋同。GB vs Probe 仍显著（+3.3pp, p=0.042）\n      是积极信号：structural advantage 在 SWE gold 上 against probe 成立。\n\n      TWV Probe 0.835 略优于 GB（+1.4pp, p=0.12, NS），与 overall gold pattern\n      一致。TWV 的优势来自 temporal smoothing 修正了部分 conf=1 边界标签，\n      这些修正恰好更贴近人工标注——但效果在小样本上不稳定。\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: SWE-Agent Gold Set Evaluation (N=328)\"\n        columns: [Model, Macro_F1, F1_stag, F1_prog, \"vs MV Δ(pp)\", \"p-value\"]\n        rows:\n          - [TWV Probe, 0.835, 0.798, 0.872, \"+2.9\", 0.06]\n          - [Structural GB, 0.821, 0.778, 0.864, \"+1.5\", 0.18]\n          - [MV Labels, 0.806, 0.762, 0.850, \"—\", \"—\"]\n          - [Structural RF, 0.804, 0.758, 0.850, \"-0.2\", 0.84]\n          - [GRU Probe, 0.788, 0.738, 0.838, \"-1.8\", 0.21]\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      SWE gold 结果补充 overall gold 分析但不改变核心叙事。建议在论文中\n      以 overall Set B（N=491, power 更强）为主呈现，SWE-specific 作为\n      supplementary analysis 放 appendix。不要以 SWE 上 GB vs MV NS 来弱化\n      Claim 1——样本量不足和 circularity 效应是更合理的解释。GB vs Probe\n      显著（p=0.042）可在正文中简要提及，强化 \"structural advantage holds\n      against neural probe on gold annotations\"。\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":44609,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":44609},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a6eb9280-ad3e-4ff8-a8df-7cd530d82ee9","uuid":"a23febbd-e1d8-4d47-adbb-af2ac08d1e4f"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":1162585,"duration_api_ms":1164152,"num_turns":1,"result":"```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Claim 4 从 \"actionable contribution\" 降级为 \"deployment analysis\"。标题改为\n  \"Structural Stagnation Detection with Deployment Analysis\"。Intervention 部分用\n  \"compute waste quantification + preliminary early termination analysis\" 措辞，\n  不宣称 \"compute-efficient early termination\"。Pareto curve 仍展示但定位为\n  \"motivating future deployment work\"。Claim 3（TLM negative）压缩到 §5 一段话 +\n  Appendix D，为 Claim 4 分析腾出 0.5 页。核心 headline 仍是 Claim 1 structural\n  dominance（+33-39pp）。2-LF 配置叙事从 \"ready-to-deploy\" 调整为\n  \"deployable with caveats — coverage-dependent on agent architecture\"。\n  补充 limitation: intervention simulation 仅 offline retrospective，online\n  deployment 留 future work。整体定位：strong detection paper with\n  promising-but-preliminary intervention analysis。\n\nimagined_experiments:\n\n  - name: compute-waste-audit\n    plan_id: compute-waste-audit\n    description: >\n      回溯分析 SWE-Agent + OpenHands 全量轨迹，量化从首次检测到停滞到轨迹结束的\n      stagnation tail 占总计算资源的比例。按 agent、解决状态、轨迹长度分层统计。\n    expected_results:\n      swe_agent:\n        n_instances: 1683\n        n_windows: 54809\n        instances_with_stag_tail: 1124\n        instances_with_stag_tail_pct: 66.8\n        mean_tail_length_steps: 11.8\n        median_tail_length_steps: 8\n        tail_compute_pct_all: 30.2\n        tail_compute_pct_unresolved: 38.5\n        tail_token_pct_all: 28.7\n        intermittent_recovery_instances: 183\n        intermittent_recovery_pct: 10.9\n      openhands:\n        n_instances: 3409\n        n_windows: 150652\n        instances_with_stag_tail: 2048\n        instances_with_stag_tail_pct: 60.1\n        mean_tail_length_steps: 15.3\n        median_tail_length_steps: 10\n        tail_compute_pct_all: 24.1\n        tail_compute_pct_unresolved: 31.8\n        tail_token_pct_all: 22.8\n        intermittent_recovery_instances: 487\n        intermittent_recovery_pct: 14.3\n      combined:\n        stagnation_tail_compute_pct: 27.3\n        stagnation_tail_token_pct: 25.8\n        median_tail_length_steps: 9\n        resolved_tail_compute_pct: 2.1\n        unresolved_tail_compute_pct: 35.4\n    result_rationale: >\n      Median 档位下 stagnation tail 存在但不够 dramatic（27% vs 乐观档 35-45%），\n      核心原因是 intermittent stagnation：约 11-14% 的轨迹在短暂停滞后恢复，\n      拉低了整体 waste 比例。SWE-Agent tail 更重（30.2%）因为 stag_rate 更高\n      （28.46% vs 21.45%）且轨迹更短（平均 33 步 vs 44 步），tail 占比更大。\n      OH tail 更轻（24.1%）因为 cmd_rep firing rate 仅 5.39%，部分停滞检测\n      延迟或遗漏。已解决轨迹的 tail 极小（2.1%）因为成功轨迹几乎不停滞\n      （error analysis 中 resolved error rate 仅 1.3%）。OH intermittent recovery\n      更高（14.3% vs SWE 10.9%）反映 OH agent 架构更倾向于 tool-switching 式\n      的\"假恢复\"——切换工具后短暂产生 progress 信号，但很快再次停滞。\n      整体 27% 足以说明问题存在，但不足以支撑 \"dramatic compute waste\" 的强主张。\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Compute Waste Audit by Agent and Resolution Status\"\n        columns: [Agent, Subset, N_instances, Tail_pct_instances, Tail_compute_pct, Tail_token_pct, Median_tail_steps]\n        rows:\n          - [SWE-Agent, All, 1683, \"66.8%\", \"30.2%\", \"28.7%\", 8]\n          - [SWE-Agent, Unresolved, \"~1599\", \"70.2%\", \"38.5%\", \"36.1%\", 9]\n          - [SWE-Agent, Resolved, \"~84\", \"8.3%\", \"2.1%\", \"1.9%\", 2]\n          - [OpenHands, All, 3409, \"60.1%\", \"24.1%\", \"22.8%\", 10]\n          - [OpenHands, Unresolved, \"~3239\", \"63.2%\", \"31.8%\", \"30.1%\", 11]\n          - [OpenHands, Resolved, \"~170\", \"5.9%\", \"1.8%\", \"1.6%\", 2]\n          - [Combined, All, 5092, \"62.3%\", \"27.3%\", \"25.8%\", 9]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Histogram: stagnation tail length distribution (steps)，双峰——大量轨迹无\n        tail (0 步) + 长尾分布。标注 median=9, mean=13.2。SWE vs OH 颜色区分。\n      - >\n        CDF: cumulative compute waste 随 tail 长度的累积分布，\n        展示约 63% 的轨迹有非零 tail，前 20% 最长 tail 贡献 ~55% 的总 waste。\n    analysis: >\n      27% compute waste 是 publishable 但 non-dramatic 的数字。论文中用\n      \"approximately one-quarter of agent compute is consumed after stagnation onset\"\n      措辞，避免 \"massive\" 或 \"dramatic\"。Intermittent recovery 本身有叙事价值：\n      证明 agent stagnation 不总是单调的，early termination 必须处理假阳性风险。\n      限制：tail 定义依赖 LF labels，gold set 上可能有偏差。\n\n  - name: early-termination-simulation\n    plan_id: early-termination-simulation\n    description: >\n      在全量轨迹上模拟不同 consecutive-stagnation threshold k 的 early stopping\n      policy，使用 structural GB 检测器的预测作为 trigger，量化每个 k 的\n      compute savings 和 task success preservation。\n    expected_results:\n      detector: structural_gb\n      total_trajectories: 5092\n      total_resolved: 255\n      thresholds:\n        k1:\n          compute_saved_pct: 28.3\n          task_success_preserved_pct: 77.6\n          n_trajectories_terminated: 3210\n          n_resolved_terminated: 57\n        k2:\n          compute_saved_pct: 22.1\n          task_success_preserved_pct: 84.3\n          n_trajectories_terminated: 2650\n          n_resolved_terminated: 40\n        k3:\n          compute_saved_pct: 17.8\n          task_success_preserved_pct: 89.4\n          n_trajectories_terminated: 2140\n          n_resolved_terminated: 27\n        k4:\n          compute_saved_pct: 14.6\n          task_success_preserved_pct: 91.8\n          n_trajectories_terminated: 1780\n          n_resolved_terminated: 21\n        k5:\n          compute_saved_pct: 12.2\n          task_success_preserved_pct: 93.3\n          n_trajectories_terminated: 1490\n          n_resolved_terminated: 17\n        k7:\n          compute_saved_pct: 7.8\n          task_success_preserved_pct: 96.1\n          n_trajectories_terminated: 1050\n          n_resolved_terminated: 10\n        k10:\n          compute_saved_pct: 4.1\n          task_success_preserved_pct: 98.4\n          n_trajectories_terminated: 620\n          n_resolved_terminated: 4\n      sweet_spot_k: 3-4\n      headline: \"k=3: 17.8% compute saved, 89.4% task success preserved\"\n      oracle_comparison:\n        oracle_k3_saved: 22.5\n        oracle_k3_preserved: 100.0\n      random_comparison:\n        random_at_18pct_saved_preserved: 65.2\n      by_agent:\n        swe_k3:\n          compute_saved_pct: 20.4\n          task_success_preserved_pct: 91.2\n        oh_k3:\n          compute_saved_pct: 16.3\n          task_success_preserved_pct: 88.1\n    result_rationale: >\n      Median 核心特征是 precision-recall 曲线较陡——sweet spot 窄。三个因素决定\n      了这一形态：\n\n      (1) Intermittent stagnation 使低 k 假阳性飙升：约 22% 的已解决轨迹在解题\n      过程中经历至少 1 个停滞窗口（agent 暂时卡住后恢复），k=1 时这些全被终止。\n      k=3 仅终止有 3+ 连续停滞的已解决轨迹（~10.6%），k=5 进一步过滤到 ~6.7%。\n      但从 k=2 到 k=4，每增加 1 仅减少 ~3% 假阳性，同时损失 ~4pp savings——\n      曲线在此区间非常陡。\n\n      (2) OH 上检测延迟拉低 savings：OH cmd_rep firing rate 5.39% 意味着部分停滞\n      检测依赖 file_diff（17.78% fire rate），但 file_diff 的 temporal lag 更大\n      （leading -4.9 步 vs SWE -1.2 步 的差异），检测晚于实际停滞 onset → tail\n      已部分消耗后才触发终止。OH 在 k=3 时仅 16.3% savings vs SWE 20.4%。\n\n      (3) 中等长度轨迹最难判断：error analysis 显示 middle segment error rate\n      10.2%（vs endpoints 6.5%），这些正是 early termination 最频繁触发的区间。\n      中等长度轨迹的停滞往往是\"exploration 与 stagnation 的边界\"，检测器的\n      confidence 较低（mean prob 0.776 on missed stagnation）。\n\n      与 oracle 对比：oracle 在 k=3 可节省 22.5% + 保留 100%，gap 来自\n      检测器假阳性。Random 在 18% savings 下仅保留 65.2%——structural 远超随机。\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Early Termination Simulation (Structural GB Detector)\"\n        columns: [k, Saved_pct, Preserved_pct, N_Terminated, Resolved_Killed, False_Term_Rate]\n        rows:\n          - [1, 28.3, 77.6, 3210, 57, \"22.4%\"]\n          - [2, 22.1, 84.3, 2650, 40, \"15.7%\"]\n          - [3, 17.8, 89.4, 2140, 27, \"10.6%\"]\n          - [4, 14.6, 91.8, 1780, 21, \"8.2%\"]\n          - [5, 12.2, 93.3, 1490, 17, \"6.7%\"]\n          - [7, 7.8, 96.1, 1050, 10, \"3.9%\"]\n          - [10, 4.1, 98.4, 620, 4, \"1.6%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Trade-off curve: x 轴 compute saved (%)，y 轴 task success preserved (%)。\n        每个 k 一个点，连线展示 trade-off。标注 sweet spot k=3-4 区间。\n        同一图上叠加 random baseline（对角线）和 oracle（近水平线）。\n        曲线较陡是 median 的视觉特征——k=2 到 k=4 之间斜率最大。\n    analysis: >\n      k=3 的 17.8% / 89.4% 需要大幅弱化措辞。原 \"compute-efficient early\n      termination\" 改为 \"preliminary analysis showing moderate savings potential\"。\n      陡峭的 trade-off 曲线是 finding 而非 weakness：说明 early termination 需要\n      精细的 threshold 调优和 domain-specific calibration，不是 drop-in solution。\n      论文建议呈现完整曲线并讨论 tradeoff landscape，而非宣称某个 operating point。\n      SWE vs OH 的差异（20.4% vs 16.3% at k=3）额外说明 savings 是 agent-dependent。\n\n  - name: cost-effectiveness-pareto\n    plan_id: cost-effectiveness-pareto\n    description: >\n      Pareto 前沿：compute saved (%) vs task success preserved (%)，对比\n      structural detector、oracle（outcome + detector）、random、LLM judge。\n      计算 normalized AUC 量化各方法的 cost-effectiveness。\n    expected_results:\n      pareto_auc_normalized:\n        structural_gb: 0.70\n        oracle_outcome: 0.97\n        oracle_detector: 0.86\n        random: 0.50\n        llm_judge_best: 0.56\n        probe_zeroshot: 0.52\n      structural_pareto_points:\n        - {k: 10, saved_pct: 4.1, preserved_pct: 98.4}\n        - {k: 7, saved_pct: 7.8, preserved_pct: 96.1}\n        - {k: 5, saved_pct: 12.2, preserved_pct: 93.3}\n        - {k: 4, saved_pct: 14.6, preserved_pct: 91.8}\n        - {k: 3, saved_pct: 17.8, preserved_pct: 89.4}\n        - {k: 2, saved_pct: 22.1, preserved_pct: 84.3}\n        - {k: 1, saved_pct: 28.3, preserved_pct: 77.6}\n      oracle_outcome_point: {saved_pct: 95.0, preserved_pct: 100.0}\n      structural_vs_random_auc_delta: 0.20\n      structural_vs_oracle_outcome_gap: 0.27\n      structural_vs_oracle_detector_gap: 0.16\n    result_rationale: >\n      Pareto AUC 0.70 处于 median 范围 [0.62, 0.78] 的中间，反映 structural\n      detector 在 detection-to-intervention 转化上 \"有价值但有限\" 的定位。\n\n      AUC 0.70 的分解：(1) 在保守区间（k≥5，savings≤12%），structural 接近 oracle\n      detector（差距 <3pp），因为只有最明显的长尾停滞被终止，假阳性极低；\n      (2) 在激进区间（k≤2，savings≥22%），structural 快速退化（与 oracle 差距\n      >15pp），因为 intermittent stagnation 导致大量假阳性终止；(3) 在 sweet spot\n      区间（k=3-4），structural 在 oracle 和 random 之间偏上方——这是 0.70 AUC\n      的主要贡献区间。\n\n      与其他方法对比：(a) Oracle detector（完美停滞检测但不知结果）AUC=0.86——\n      即使检测完美，k-consecutive 策略仍有损失，因为部分已解决轨迹确实经历真实\n      停滞；(b) Oracle outcome（知道结果）AUC=0.97——接近完美，gap 仅来自极端\n      truncation 点；(c) LLM judge AUC=0.56——检测精度低（best 0.80 vs structural\n      0.97）导致 Pareto 前沿被压缩到接近 random；(d) Probe zero-shot AUC=0.52——\n      cross-agent 检测能力差使其在 intervention 场景下几乎等同于 random。\n\n      Structural vs random 的 0.20 AUC gap 证明检测质量直接转化为干预质量，但\n      structural vs oracle detector 的 0.16 gap 指向改进空间：更好的 intermittent\n      stagnation 处理（如 adaptive k 或 cooldown period）可能显著提升 Pareto 前沿。\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Pareto AUC Comparison\"\n        columns: [Method, AUC_normalized, vs_Random, vs_Oracle_Outcome]\n        rows:\n          - [Oracle (outcome), 0.97, \"+0.47\", \"-\"]\n          - [Oracle (detector), 0.86, \"+0.36\", \"-0.11\"]\n          - [Structural GB, 0.70, \"+0.20\", \"-0.27\"]\n          - [LLM Judge (best), 0.56, \"+0.06\", \"-0.41\"]\n          - [Probe (zero-shot), 0.52, \"+0.02\", \"-0.45\"]\n          - [Random, 0.50, \"-\", \"-0.47\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Pareto frontier figure（Claim 4 核心 figure）：x 轴 Compute Saved (%)，\n        y 轴 Task Success Preserved (%)。六条曲线/线：Oracle-outcome（近水平线，\n        在 x=95% 处陡降），Oracle-detector（略低曲线），Structural GB（中间曲线，\n        k 值标注），LLM Judge best（接近对角线但略高），Probe zero-shot（近乎\n        与 random 重合），Random（精确对角线）。阴影区域标注 structural vs\n        random 的优势面积。图例标注 AUC 值。整体传达：structural 有实际价值\n        但远非 oracle——定位为 \"motivating future work\"。\n    analysis: >\n      Pareto 图是 Claim 4 在 median 下的核心呈现。0.70 AUC 的定位：\"our\n      structural detector provides a meaningful advantage over uninformed baselines\n      for early termination, but substantial room remains between current performance\n      and the theoretical optimum.\" 图中 LLM judge 和 probe 接近 random 的\n      表现反向强化 Claim 1：structural features 不仅在 detection 上 dominate，\n      在 intervention 场景下的优势更加 dramatic。建议将 Pareto 图放在 §5 而非\n      §4，与 compute waste audit 同一小节，标题为 \"Deployment Analysis\"。\n\n  - name: 2lf-cross-agent\n    plan_id: 2lf-cross-agent\n    registry_id: e1-2lf-crossagent\n    description: >\n      2-LF deployable config（cmd_rep + file_diff，无 OB）在 OH 上的跨 agent\n      性能评估。验证无 privileged outcome 信息时的部署可行性和 coverage 限制。\n    expected_results:\n      structural_gb:\n        oh_2lf_conf2:\n          macro_f1: 0.960\n          f1_stag: 0.941\n          f1_prog: 0.979\n          n_eval: 4017\n        oh_3lf_conf2_with_2lf_model:\n          macro_f1: 0.945\n          f1_stag: 0.918\n          f1_prog: 0.972\n          n_eval: 43376\n        oh_all_with_2lf_model:\n          macro_f1: 0.940\n          f1_stag: 0.908\n          f1_prog: 0.972\n          n_eval: 150652\n      structural_rf:\n        oh_2lf_conf2:\n          macro_f1: 0.968\n          f1_stag: 0.952\n          f1_prog: 0.984\n          n_eval: 4017\n      coverage:\n        oh_2lf_conf2_windows: 4017\n        oh_2lf_conf2_pct: 2.7\n        oh_3lf_conf2_windows: 43376\n        oh_3lf_conf2_pct: 28.8\n        coverage_ratio_2lf_vs_3lf: 0.093\n      label_consistency:\n        agreement_2lf_3lf_on_intersection: 1.00\n        n_intersection: 4017\n      swe_indomain_2lf_ref:\n        gb_macro_f1: 0.978\n        delta_vs_3lf_pp: -1.8\n      delta_vs_3lf_crossagent:\n        gb_oh_all_delta_pp: -2.7\n    result_rationale: >\n      2-LF cross-agent 轻微退化（-2.7pp on OH all, 0.940 vs 3-LF 0.967）的\n      根因是 OH 上 cmd_rep firing rate 仅 5.39%（vs SWE 上远高覆盖率），\n      导致两个连锁效应：\n\n      (1) Coverage 崩塌：2-LF conf≥2 仅覆盖 OH 的 2.7% 窗口（4017/150652），\n      vs 3-LF conf≥2 的 28.8%。真实部署中 2-LF config 对 OH 类型 agent 只能\n      为极少数窗口给出高置信判断。这是 agent 架构决定的——OH 使用不同的\n      tool-calling convention，重复命令以不同方式表现，cmd_rep LF 的设计未适配。\n\n      (2) 训练数据缩减：2-LF 在 SWE 上的 conf≥2 训练窗口从 15401 降到 10243\n      （-33.5%）。虽然 structural features 非常 data-efficient（10% data 仍 0.991\n      in-domain），但跨 agent 场景下更少训练数据 + 更窄的 LF coverage 共同导致\n      decision boundary 略有偏移。\n\n      Label consistency 100% 是关键正面信号：在 cmd_rep 和 file_diff 都触发的\n      4017 窗口上，2-LF 和 3-LF 标签完全一致——退化纯粹是 coverage 问题。\n      2-LF 在 SWE in-domain（0.978, -1.8pp）几乎无退化，因为 SWE cmd_rep\n      覆盖率足够。跨 agent 退化完全是 agent 架构差异导致的 LF 行为差异。\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: 2-LF vs 3-LF Cross-Agent Performance\"\n        columns: [Config, Eval_Set, N_eval, GB_F1, RF_F1, Coverage]\n        rows:\n          - [3-LF, OH conf≥2, 43376, 0.992, 0.996, \"28.8%\"]\n          - [2-LF, OH 2-LF conf≥2, 4017, 0.960, 0.968, \"2.7%\"]\n          - [2-LF, OH 3-LF conf≥2, 43376, 0.945, 0.938, \"28.8%\"]\n          - [2-LF, OH all, 150652, 0.940, 0.925, \"100%\"]\n          - [2-LF, SWE in-domain, 10243, 0.978, 0.978, \"66.5%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Grouped bar chart：3-LF vs 2-LF cross-agent GB macro_f1，旁边标注\n        coverage bars（第二 y 轴）。视觉突出 performance 仅小幅退化但\n        coverage 差距巨大（28.8% vs 2.7%）。\n    analysis: >\n      2-LF 叙事从 \"ready-to-deploy\" 改为 \"deployable with caveats —\n      coverage-dependent on agent architecture\"。核心 caveat：2-LF config 的\n      实用性取决于 cmd_rep 在目标 agent 上的 firing pattern。SWE-Agent 类型\n      （cmd_rep 高覆盖）可近乎无损部署；OH 类型（cmd_rep 低覆盖）仅 2.7%\n      窗口获得高置信判断。这不是 detection quality 问题而是 LF design 问题——\n      建议 future work 设计 agent-agnostic LFs。与 Claim 4 关系：early termination\n      simulation 的 savings 数字基于 3-LF detector，真实部署用 2-LF 时 OH\n      savings 会更低——需在 limitation 中声明。\n\n  - name: matched-scope-eval\n    plan_id: matched-scope-eval\n    registry_id: e2-gb-matched-scope\n    description: >\n      Matched-scope 对比：GB structural 和 Probe zero-shot 在完全相同的 OH\n      窗口子集上评估，控制 prevalence 和评估条件，给出公平对比下的 delta。\n    expected_results:\n      matched_subset:\n        n_windows: 6122\n        stag_prevalence: 0.193\n        stag_windows: 1182\n        prog_windows: 4940\n        selection: \"stratified sample from OH 3-LF labeled windows, prevalence-matched to OH overall\"\n      gb_structural:\n        macro_f1: 0.970\n        f1_stag: 0.951\n        f1_prog: 0.989\n        accuracy: 0.978\n      probe_zeroshot:\n        macro_f1: 0.598\n        f1_stag: 0.308\n        f1_prog: 0.888\n        accuracy: 0.672\n      delta_pp: 37.2\n      bootstrap_ci_95: [34.1, 40.3]\n      bootstrap_p: 0.0001\n      mcnemar_chi2: 1823\n      mcnemar_p: 0.0\n      ref_comparison:\n        full_oh_gb: 0.9674\n        full_oh_probe_zs: 0.5984\n        full_oh_delta_pp: 36.9\n        matched_vs_full_delta_diff_pp: 0.3\n    result_rationale: >\n      Matched-scope delta 37.2pp 基本确认 full-set 的 36.9pp（差异仅 0.3pp），\n      处于 median 范围 [33, 39] 偏上方。\n\n      Delta 稳定性的原因：(1) Probe zero-shot 的失败是 systematic 的——SWE 上\n      训练的 embedding space 在 OH 上无法对应 stagnation boundary，无论子集\n      选择如何，probe 都在 0.59-0.61 范围内波动；(2) GB 的 cross-agent 性能\n      （0.967-0.970）在不同子集上也很稳定，因为 structural features 的跨域\n      迁移基于 domain-invariant 模式（重复率、文件变化率等），不依赖特定窗口。\n\n      Median 档位下 delta 未达到计划中的 39.4pp（降低 2.2pp）的可能原因：\n      matched-scope 的 prevalence matching（19.3% stag）略低于 conf≥2 子集的\n      stag rate，使得两种方法在 majority class 上更容易获得高分，压缩了差距。\n      但 bootstrap CI [34.1, 40.3] 不包含 0，统计显著性无悬念。McNemar chi²=1823\n      进一步确认差异在样本级别也高度显著（模型在不同窗口上犯错）。\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Matched-Scope Comparison (N=6122, stag=19.3%)\"\n        columns: [Model, Macro_F1, F1_stag, F1_prog, Accuracy]\n        rows:\n          - [Structural GB (zero-shot), 0.970, 0.951, 0.989, 0.978]\n          - [GRU Probe (zero-shot), 0.598, 0.308, 0.888, 0.672]\n          - [\"Δ (pp)\", \"+37.2\", \"+64.3\", \"+10.1\", \"+30.6\"]\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      +37.2pp 是 Claim 1 的 primary headline number（替代原 +36.9pp 或 +39.4pp）。\n      Matched-scope 方法论本身是 minor contribution：本例中 full-set 和\n      matched-scope 差异很小（0.3pp），但方法论严谨性 preempt reviewer 的\n      \"unfair comparison\" 质疑。论文中在 §4 Claim 1 用 matched-scope delta\n      作为 primary comparison，footnote 说明与 full-set 一致性。\n      注意：f1_stag 差距（+64.3pp）远大于 macro_f1 差距，因为 probe 在\n      minority class（stagnation）上完全失效（zero-shot f1_stag 仅 0.308）。\n\n  - name: llm-on-oh\n    plan_id: llm-on-oh\n    description: >\n      将 LLM-as-judge baseline 扩展到 OpenHands 轨迹，验证 structural features\n      的跨 agent 优势在 LLM baseline 对比下是否保持或扩大。\n    expected_results:\n      eval_subset: \"5000 OH windows (cost-controlled random sample)\"\n      claude_zeroshot_oh:\n        macro_f1: 0.581\n        f1_stag: 0.423\n        f1_prog: 0.739\n        parse_failures: 12\n        cost_usd: 38.50\n      claude_fewshot_cot_oh:\n        macro_f1: 0.728\n        f1_stag: 0.631\n        f1_prog: 0.825\n        parse_failures: 3\n        cost_usd: 112.40\n      gpt55_zeroshot_oh:\n        macro_f1: 0.693\n        f1_stag: 0.584\n        f1_prog: 0.802\n        parse_failures: 1089\n        parse_failure_rate: 0.218\n        n_valid: 3911\n        cost_usd: 32.80\n      structural_gb_ref:\n        oh_all_macro_f1: 0.9674\n      best_llm_vs_structural_delta_pp: 23.9\n      cross_agent_degradation:\n        claude_zs_degradation_pp: -8.2\n        claude_fscot_degradation_pp: -7.1\n        gpt55_zs_degradation_pp: -5.4\n        structural_gb_degradation_pp: -2.8\n      swe_ref:\n        best_llm_swe: 0.7994\n        structural_swe: 0.9951\n        swe_delta_pp: 16.8\n    result_rationale: >\n      LLM judge 在 OH 上进一步退化（best 0.728 vs SWE 0.799，-7.1pp），\n      structural 仅退化 2.8pp（0.967 vs 0.995），使优势从 +16.8pp 扩大到 +23.9pp。\n\n      LLM 退化的三个机制：(1) Prompt-distribution shift——few-shot examples 来自\n      SWE-Agent（bash-centric, linear workflow），OH 使用不同 tool-calling\n      convention（GUI interaction, multi-tool orchestration），prompt 中的停滞\n      模式描述不完全适用。Claude few-shot-cot 受影响最大因为其 few-shot\n      examples 产生最强的 SWE-specific 偏置。(2) Context dilution——OH 轨迹\n      平均 44 步 vs SWE 33 步，更长的输入稀释 LLM 对关键停滞信号的注意力。\n      (3) Stagnation diversity——OH 的停滞模式不仅是命令重复，还有不同工具间的\n      无效切换、配置反复修改等 subtle 模式，zero-shot prompt 的覆盖面不足。\n\n      Structural features 的鲁棒性（仅 -2.8pp）来自其 domain-invariant 设计：\n      重复率、文件变化率、输出长度模式等特征不依赖 agent-specific 的 action\n      语义。GPT-5.5 的 21.8% parse failure rate 与 SWE（22.4%）一致——是模型\n      compliance 问题，非 domain-specific。\n\n      +23.9pp 在 OH 上的扩大进一步强化了 Claim 1 的核心论点：structural\n      features 的迁移优势在更 diverse 的 agent 上更 pronounced。\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: LLM Judge Cross-Agent Extension\"\n        columns: [Model, Config, SWE_F1, OH_F1, Degradation_pp, Cost_USD]\n        rows:\n          - [Claude, zero-shot, 0.663, 0.581, -8.2, 38.50]\n          - [Claude, few-shot-cot, 0.799, 0.728, -7.1, 112.40]\n          - [GPT-5.5, zero-shot, 0.747, 0.693, -5.4, 32.80]\n          - [Structural GB, \"—\", 0.995, 0.967, -2.8, 0]\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      OH 结果建议与 SWE 合并呈现，一个表展示 cross-agent degradation pattern。\n      +23.9pp 可作为 OH-specific number，但 primary comparison 仍用 SWE +16.8pp\n      （有完整 multi-config 消融）。OH LLM 数据的主要叙事价值：证明 structural\n      advantage 在更 diverse 的 agent 上 不减反增，排除 \"structural advantage\n      is SWE-specific\" 的 reviewer concern。LLM cost 论证不受 circular eval\n      影响——这是 caveat-free 的优势。\n\n  - name: e4-gb-swe-gold\n    plan_id: e4-gb-swe-gold\n    registry_id: e4-gb-swe-gold\n    description: >\n      在 gold 3-annotator 标注集的 SWE-Agent 子集上单独评估 GB structural features，\n      验证 structural advantage 在 SWE 人工标注上是否成立。\n    expected_results:\n      swe_gold_subset:\n        n_windows: 328\n        n_stag: 108\n        n_prog: 220\n        stag_prevalence: 0.329\n      per_model:\n        twv_probe:\n          macro_f1: 0.835\n          f1_stag: 0.798\n          f1_prog: 0.872\n        gb:\n          macro_f1: 0.821\n          f1_stag: 0.778\n          f1_prog: 0.864\n        mv:\n          macro_f1: 0.806\n          f1_stag: 0.762\n          f1_prog: 0.850\n        rf:\n          macro_f1: 0.804\n          f1_stag: 0.758\n          f1_prog: 0.850\n        probe:\n          macro_f1: 0.788\n          f1_stag: 0.738\n          f1_prog: 0.838\n      bootstrap_tests:\n        gb_vs_mv:\n          delta_pp: 1.5\n          p: 0.18\n          significant: false\n        gb_vs_probe:\n          delta_pp: 3.3\n          p: 0.042\n          significant: true\n        twv_probe_vs_gb:\n          delta_pp: 1.4\n          p: 0.12\n          significant: false\n      ref_overall_gold_set_b:\n        gb: 0.8394\n        delta_swe_vs_overall_pp: -1.8\n    result_rationale: >\n      SWE-specific gold F1 0.821 在 median 范围 [0.80, 0.84] 的中间。\n\n      比 overall Set B（0.839）低 1.8pp 的原因：(1) SWE gold 子集的 stag\n      prevalence 更高（32.9% vs overall ~24.8%），更多 stagnation 样本意味着\n      recall_stag 受到更大考验——从 f1-stag-analysis 已知 recall_stag 是\n      performance 瓶颈（conf≥2 仅 0.77）。(2) SWE 上 MV-gold agreement 更低\n      （gold-set-eval-v1 显示 SWE 0.735 vs OH 0.778），说明 SWE 的 LF labels\n      与人工判断 diverge 更大——可能因为 SWE 有更多 \"semantic stagnation\"\n      （agent 在做不同的事但都无效），人类标注者更容易识别但 LF 难以捕获。\n      (3) GB 训练在 LF labels 上，SWE gold 上的 1.8pp drop 反映了 LF 与人工\n      标注在 SWE-specific 边界案例上的分歧。\n\n      GB vs MV 差异不显著（+1.5pp, p=0.18）vs overall 上显著（+4.2pp, p=0.0001）\n      ——primary 原因是 power 不足：328 vs 491 windows。Effect size 也更小\n      （1.5pp vs 4.2pp），可能因为 SWE 上 GB 本质更接近 LF 重建（circular eval\n      程度更高），使得 GB 和 MV 行为更趋同。GB vs Probe 仍显著（+3.3pp, p=0.042）\n      是积极信号：structural advantage 在 SWE gold 上 against probe 成立。\n\n      TWV Probe 0.835 略优于 GB（+1.4pp, p=0.12, NS），与 overall gold pattern\n      一致。TWV 的优势来自 temporal smoothing 修正了部分 conf=1 边界标签，\n      这些修正恰好更贴近人工标注——但效果在小样本上不稳定。\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: SWE-Agent Gold Set Evaluation (N=328)\"\n        columns: [Model, Macro_F1, F1_stag, F1_prog, \"vs MV Δ(pp)\", \"p-value\"]\n        rows:\n          - [TWV Probe, 0.835, 0.798, 0.872, \"+2.9\", 0.06]\n          - [Structural GB, 0.821, 0.778, 0.864, \"+1.5\", 0.18]\n          - [MV Labels, 0.806, 0.762, 0.850, \"—\", \"—\"]\n          - [Structural RF, 0.804, 0.758, 0.850, \"-0.2\", 0.84]\n          - [GRU Probe, 0.788, 0.738, 0.838, \"-1.8\", 0.21]\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      SWE gold 结果补充 overall gold 分析但不改变核心叙事。建议在论文中\n      以 overall Set B（N=491, power 更强）为主呈现，SWE-specific 作为\n      supplementary analysis 放 appendix。不要以 SWE 上 GB vs MV NS 来弱化\n      Claim 1——样本量不足和 circularity 效应是更合理的解释。GB vs Probe\n      显著（p=0.042）可在正文中简要提及，强化 \"structural advantage holds\n      against neural probe on gold annotations\"。\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"a6eb9280-ad3e-4ff8-a8df-7cd530d82ee9","total_cost_usd":1.69333875,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":44609,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"output_tokens":54757,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":44609,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":54757,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation_input_tokens":44609,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":44609},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":39572,"outputTokens":21,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.039677000000000004,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":54757,"cacheReadInputTokens":11841,"cacheCreationInputTokens":44609,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.65366175,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"827a84ad-32aa-44f2-b714-2c07e6e8158b"}
